WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.443 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.443 --> 00:00:02.867 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:02.867 --> 00:00:03.367 align:middle line:90%


00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:04.329 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.329 --> 00:00:12.297 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.297 --> 00:00:13.880 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Let's
get right into it.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.790 align:middle line:84%
There's a bunch that I want to
try to catch up from yesterday.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.870 align:middle line:84%
You've just turned
in paper number one.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.140 align:middle line:84%
But I'm a big fan of trying
to have people do work,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.280 align:middle line:84%
like do the daily
work in the class,

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.280 align:middle line:84%
thinking towards what the
major assignments are.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.640 align:middle line:84%
So now, I have to tell you
about paper number two.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.500 align:middle line:84%
For paper number
two, you are going

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.270 align:middle line:84%
to identify a specific
learning environment.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.980 align:middle line:84%
You're going to identify
the Physics Department

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.100 align:middle line:84%
at MIT, your middle school,
your elementary school,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.990 align:middle line:84%
your high school World
Languages Department,

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.840 align:middle line:84%
an after school program you work
with a library but something

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.970 align:middle line:84%
very specific, not like
schools in general.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.260 align:middle line:90%
A place.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.370 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to reflect
upon a particular education

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.160 align:middle line:90%
technology.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.820 align:middle line:84%
And you are either going
to recommend or not

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.982 align:middle line:84%
recommend that they adopt
this particular technology.

00:01:04.982 --> 00:01:06.440 align:middle line:84%
So part of the
argument that you'll

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.130 align:middle line:84%
read about in, hopefully,
the first half of Failure

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:13.490 align:middle line:84%
to Disrupt, is that
while we can get

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:17.400 align:middle line:84%
a general sense of the efficacy
of different technologies--

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.450 align:middle line:84%
we could say, this
class of technologies

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.490 align:middle line:84%
seems to have better
research evidence behind it

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.787 align:middle line:84%
than this class of
technologies-- there's really

00:01:24.787 --> 00:01:26.370 align:middle line:84%
nothing that anyone
has ever invented,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.460 align:middle line:84%
which is really every
school or every department

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.910 align:middle line:84%
ought to be using X.
All of those decisions

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:36.360 align:middle line:84%
have to do with particular
times, particular places,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.760 align:middle line:90%
particular people.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.640 align:middle line:84%
So what you'll have to do
is identify some technology.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.830 align:middle line:84%
Classify it like what genre is
it from, or what is it like.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.580 align:middle line:90%
What are its potential benefits?

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:48.750 align:middle line:90%
What are its affordances?

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.890 align:middle line:90%
What are its limitations?

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.130 align:middle line:84%
And then what is the
characteristic of the place

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.480 align:middle line:90%
that you're recommending it to.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.440 align:middle line:84%
And do the benefits, and
needs, and characteristics

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.680 align:middle line:84%
of a place all line up, such
that you recommend adoption?

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.540 align:middle line:84%
Or, do they not line
up such that you

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.550 align:middle line:90%
don't recommend adoption?

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:09.240 align:middle line:84%
It should be a memo directed
to a particular stakeholder--

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.900 align:middle line:84%
a department head, an assistant
superintendent for curriculum

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.070 align:middle line:84%
and instruction, the
library director,

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.920 align:middle line:84%
the President of the Boys and
Girls Club, whatever it is.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.160 align:middle line:84%
I think I had one
student who was

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.290 align:middle line:84%
building a program for
Ukrainian refugees last year.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.130 align:middle line:84%
And he basically wrote a memo
to himself, which was fine.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:28.905 align:middle line:84%
On pages 74 through 76, in
Failure to Disrupt which--

00:02:28.905 --> 00:02:30.030 align:middle line:90%
at least in the hardcover--

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.383 align:middle line:84%
I think it's the same
in the paperback--

00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:34.800 align:middle line:84%
there's a section called What
a Department Head Should Do,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.020 align:middle line:84%
which is not exactly
this assignment,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.540 align:middle line:84%
but a pretty close
worked example.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.400 align:middle line:90%


00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.970 align:middle line:84%
So I think that
specific example is

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.320 align:middle line:84%
about using intelligent
tutors in math class.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.737 align:middle line:84%
So you should pick
something other

00:02:47.737 --> 00:02:50.780 align:middle line:84%
than that specific
example for you to use.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:55.040 align:middle line:84%
But you should think
about doing things,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.187 align:middle line:84%
like assessing the claims
that the developers

00:02:58.187 --> 00:03:00.770 align:middle line:84%
of this technology make, seeing
what kind of research evidence

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.680 align:middle line:84%
there is around that,
seeing under what context

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.920 align:middle line:84%
this seems to work or not, what
kind of enabling conditions

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.810 align:middle line:84%
are required, whether or not
those enabling conditions exist.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.560 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about all
these kinds of things

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:12.890 align:middle line:90%
over the next few weeks.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.772 align:middle line:84%
So you don't have
to understand what

00:03:15.772 --> 00:03:16.980 align:middle line:90%
you're going to do right now.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.730 align:middle line:84%
But I you to have a
sense of this assignment.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:23.240 align:middle line:84%
For some of you, you
will pick a context

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.933 align:middle line:84%
that you can work with that
you actually know a ton about.

00:03:26.933 --> 00:03:29.100 align:middle line:84%
Or, like even if you didn't
know something about it,

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.260 align:middle line:84%
you would be able to like,
oh, the head of AeroAstro

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:34.560 align:middle line:90%
is a good colleague of mine.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.080 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to just go ask him
what he thinks about this.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.220 align:middle line:84%
Or, like what capacity
are and things like that.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.900 align:middle line:84%
There may be other things
that you need to fabricate,

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.650 align:middle line:84%
which you would be
allowed to fabricate.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.470 align:middle line:84%
You just have to somewhere
stipulate along the way

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.470 align:middle line:90%
that you're fabricating them.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:46.970 align:middle line:90%
So I know.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.160 align:middle line:84%
I have to be like
the characteristics

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.798 align:middle line:84%
of the world language teachers
in my high school are this.

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.840 align:middle line:84%
And you might not actually
know who the new staff

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:54.882 align:middle line:90%
are and things like that.

00:03:54.882 --> 00:03:59.390 align:middle line:90%
But that would be OK.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.100 align:middle line:84%
There's pages written
about what you're

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.730 align:middle line:90%
supposed to do in the syllabus.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.910 align:middle line:84%
But does that give you a
general sense of the target

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.147 align:middle line:84%
that you should be
thinking about over the--

00:04:07.147 --> 00:04:08.730 align:middle line:84%
I don't even remember
the exact dates,

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.380 align:middle line:84%
but it's roughly
a month from now.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.900 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, each assignment takes
about a third of the class.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.530 align:middle line:90%
So does that all seem good?

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.871 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:04:17.871 --> 00:04:19.579 align:middle line:84%
Anybody have any
questions that they want

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:20.880 align:middle line:90%
to ask about that assignment?

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.900 align:middle line:90%


00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.970 align:middle line:90%
OK, let's wrap up MOOCs.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.090 align:middle line:84%
So last time we were
talking about MOOCs

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:30.710 align:middle line:90%
made these three big bets.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.270 align:middle line:84%
That they could
provide new pathways

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.610 align:middle line:84%
into higher education for
people with limited access.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.670 align:middle line:84%
That they could reorganize,
disrupt, unbundle

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:38.490 align:middle line:90%
higher education.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.972 align:middle line:84%
They could create new structures
across higher education.

00:04:40.972 --> 00:04:42.680 align:middle line:84%
And then the one that
I was most involved

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:47.420 align:middle line:84%
in is that they create a new
data driven science of learning.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.980 align:middle line:84%
Maybe part of the
second one, but maybe

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.140 align:middle line:84%
was clearly a bet
for some actors,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.200 align:middle line:84%
less so for nonprofits,
more so for for-profits

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.810 align:middle line:84%
was that you could make a whole
bunch of money doing this.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:02.960 align:middle line:84%
That has not panned out as
well as some people had hoped.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.950 align:middle line:84%
So here's an announcement
today that Accenture

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.170 align:middle line:90%
is purchasing Udacity.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:12.370 align:middle line:84%
So Udacity was the company that
was founded by Sebastian Thrun.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:13.870 align:middle line:84%
Sebastian Thrun is
one of the people

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.080 align:middle line:84%
who taught this Intro to AI
class that made the whole MOOC

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.225 align:middle line:90%
phenomenon start.

00:05:18.225 --> 00:05:21.730 align:middle line:90%


00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.570 align:middle line:84%
Accenture is like
a consulting firm,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.790 align:middle line:84%
and they're going to use
it to do AI training.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:30.140 align:middle line:84%
So this is not like
transforming all of education.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:32.410 align:middle line:84%
This is doing pretty
well-established

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.090 align:middle line:90%
executive education.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.150 align:middle line:84%
And there may be ways that you
could do executive education

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.333 align:middle line:84%
better with the MOOC
platform than you could

00:05:39.333 --> 00:05:41.500 align:middle line:84%
with other pre-existing
technologies, or strategies,

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.333 align:middle line:90%
or things like this.

00:05:42.333 --> 00:05:45.340 align:middle line:84%
But like, this is not going
to profoundly changed the way

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:46.850 align:middle line:90%
executive education unfolds.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:49.940 align:middle line:84%
This is just like somebody
who sells executive education,

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.210 align:middle line:84%
buying a potential
provider of it.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:58.900 align:middle line:84%
In the notes here, it says that
their best guess is that they--

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.850 align:middle line:84%
a deal fell through
with an asking price

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:02.300 align:middle line:90%
of about $80 million.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.910 align:middle line:84%
And so nobody knows
for sure, but they

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.090 align:middle line:84%
think that this deal was
probably about that size.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:11.990 align:middle line:84%
But Udacity took in $300
million of investment.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:14.200 align:middle line:84%
So I don't go to the
Sloan School of Management

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.200 align:middle line:90%
like some of you do.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.590 align:middle line:84%
But I'm pretty sure that
if you take $300 million

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.500 align:middle line:84%
and sell a thing for $80
million, you didn't do it right.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.460 align:middle line:84%
Or, at least your
investors don't think so.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:31.420 align:middle line:84%
But I think that's, to me, it's
a great indication of where

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.330 align:middle line:84%
this class of technologies
have settled out,

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.860 align:middle line:84%
which is like $80
million, a lot of money.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.110 align:middle line:84%
If any of you were offered
$80 million for a business,

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.250 align:middle line:84%
you'd probably be like,
yeah, that's pretty good.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.167 align:middle line:90%
I'll take $80 million.

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:43.240 align:middle line:90%
That seems great.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:49.420 align:middle line:84%
But what it's accomplishing is
like a very well established

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.300 align:middle line:84%
existing thing in
the education system.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.300 align:middle line:84%
Or, a way that I've phrased
it in the book, which

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.880 align:middle line:84%
a colleague of mine
really came up with,

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:02.000 align:middle line:84%
is that these technologies did
not disrupt education systems.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.400 align:middle line:84%
They were domesticated
by education systems.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.580 align:middle line:84%
Education systems said,
like you fit there.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.297 align:middle line:84%
Like, you, little
company, you would

00:07:08.297 --> 00:07:10.130 align:middle line:84%
be pretty good at doing
executive education.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:11.290 align:middle line:90%
Why you don't you do that?

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:14.200 align:middle line:84%
That is not at all what
Sebastian Thrun was envisioning

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.410 align:middle line:84%
when he said that there might be
only 50 educational institutions

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.990 align:middle line:84%
in higher education
in the world.

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:23.770 align:middle line:84%
But to me, this is very
representative of how

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:26.470 align:middle line:84%
I think education
technology often works.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.583 align:middle line:84%
We very rarely build things
that are totally transformative.

00:07:29.583 --> 00:07:31.000 align:middle line:84%
And we can often
build things that

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.255 align:middle line:84%
are useful in pretty
specific contexts.

00:07:33.255 --> 00:07:37.464 align:middle line:90%


00:07:37.464 --> 00:07:39.850 align:middle line:84%
I and lots of other
people had really

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:44.830 align:middle line:84%
hoped that there would be
very new kinds of science that

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:46.300 align:middle line:90%
would be possible.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.430 align:middle line:84%
So we had never really,
in higher education,

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:53.140 align:middle line:84%
had a system where you
just had, all of a sudden,

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:56.110 align:middle line:84%
millions and millions of
people doing online learning

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.450 align:middle line:84%
and all of that
was being recorded.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:01.300 align:middle line:84%
So when I got hired to
help build the research

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.690 align:middle line:84%
infrastructure for
HarvardX and MITx and EdX,

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:07.515 align:middle line:84%
one of the most exciting things
was like, all of these people

00:08:07.515 --> 00:08:08.140 align:middle line:90%
are signing up.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.220 align:middle line:90%
And they're all clicking.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:11.200 align:middle line:90%
And we're recording everything.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:15.500 align:middle line:84%
You might think of all of the
things that you did in school.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:17.000 align:middle line:84%
Probably even better,
as an example,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.570 align:middle line:84%
like think about all the
things that I did in school.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.280 align:middle line:84%
So I filled out
zillions of worksheets.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:23.890 align:middle line:90%
I typed all kinds of papers.

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:25.900 align:middle line:84%
All that stuff just ended
up in my mom's attic

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:27.230 align:middle line:90%
and was eventually thrown away.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:29.620 align:middle line:84%
The whole records of
my learning were lost.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.450 align:middle line:84%
But young people today
have their records

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:34.190 align:middle line:84%
of learning stored in all
of these online platforms.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.820 align:middle line:84%
If you saw what
millions of learners

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:40.750 align:middle line:84%
were doing in really granular,
detailed ways over days,

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:42.610 align:middle line:84%
or months, or
years, shouldn't you

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.953 align:middle line:84%
be able to figure out all
kinds of exciting things

00:08:44.953 --> 00:08:46.870 align:middle line:84%
about the science of
learning, about like what

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:48.330 align:middle line:84%
are the most effective
teaching techniques,

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:49.400 align:middle line:90%
how do people learn best?

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.590 align:middle line:84%
All kinds of things
that might be helpless.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:56.850 align:middle line:84%
And it proved to be much, much
harder than people expected.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:00.080 align:middle line:84%
I would say, the core reason
why it proved to be really hard

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.750 align:middle line:84%
is that you can have terabytes
of data about what people click,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:07.730 align:middle line:84%
and still not really know
what's going on in their heads.

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:11.210 align:middle line:84%
What people click on a screen
or what they add as an answer

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:14.450 align:middle line:84%
to a problem doesn't necessarily
tell you all that much

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:17.540 align:middle line:84%
about the cognition that they
went through to get there.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:20.030 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't tell you a
whole lot about which

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.370 align:middle line:84%
parts of the instruction that
they receive to that point

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.080 align:middle line:90%
helped them do that.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.790 align:middle line:84%
For years, there
was a whole class

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:29.733 align:middle line:84%
of papers that just correlated
everything to everything else.

00:09:29.733 --> 00:09:30.900 align:middle line:90%
How many times do you click?

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.660 align:middle line:90%
How many times did you log in?

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.080 align:middle line:90%
How many units did you do?

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.427 align:middle line:90%
How far apart they were?

00:09:35.427 --> 00:09:36.510 align:middle line:90%
All these kinds of things.

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:39.830 align:middle line:84%
And basically, like there
was a whole series of papers

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:41.210 align:middle line:84%
that were all
published that were

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:44.690 align:middle line:84%
all along the lines of people
who do more stuff do better

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:47.283 align:middle line:90%
than people who do less stuff.

00:09:47.283 --> 00:09:49.450 align:middle line:84%
People who clicked more
often in the systems, people

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:52.510 align:middle line:84%
who watched all the videos,
did better on the problems.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:54.010 align:middle line:84%
People who did all
the problems were

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.700 align:middle line:90%
more likely to pass the course.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:58.420 align:middle line:84%
People who logged in more time,
people who spent more hours

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.075 align:middle line:84%
were more likely
to pass the course.

00:10:00.075 --> 00:10:02.110 align:middle line:90%
None of that is interesting.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:04.330 align:middle line:84%
You didn't need a $60
million research enterprise

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:07.210 align:middle line:84%
to figure out that doing more
stuff helps people learn.

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:11.530 align:middle line:84%
There are a few things
that we did that--

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.080 align:middle line:84%
and I'll talk about them more
as the semester goes on-- that

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.510 align:middle line:90%
tried to go beyond that.

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:18.130 align:middle line:84%
There were a bunch
of us who were

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:21.970 align:middle line:84%
really interested in
this idea that like--

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:23.470 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of people
have done research

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.800 align:middle line:84%
that suggests that the attitudes
that you bring to a learning

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.340 align:middle line:84%
experience really matter for
your success in that learning

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:31.450 align:middle line:90%
experience.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:34.450 align:middle line:84%
And of you ever heard
of stereotype threat?

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:36.280 align:middle line:84%
So stereotype
threat is this idea

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:40.240 align:middle line:84%
that if we trigger
stereotypes that people

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.640 align:middle line:84%
have as they enter a
learning environment

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:44.680 align:middle line:84%
or as they're in a
learning environment,

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:48.230 align:middle line:84%
those stereotypes can
negatively impact them.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:52.515 align:middle line:84%
So if you ask people to describe
their gender before a math test,

00:10:52.515 --> 00:10:53.890 align:middle line:84%
then people who
identify as girls

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:55.800 align:middle line:90%
are more likely to do less well.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.300 align:middle line:84%
You can do the same thing in
different racial configurations

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:00.220 align:middle line:84%
and other kinds of
things like that.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.998 align:middle line:84%
And the way that you can
inoculate people, in theory,

00:11:02.998 --> 00:11:04.540 align:middle line:84%
against some of
these kinds of things

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:07.390 align:middle line:84%
is by emphasizing
how they belong.

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.432 align:middle line:84%
Is by saying, like
you belong in here.

00:11:09.432 --> 00:11:11.390 align:middle line:84%
Like, you have a space,
you deserve to be here.

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:14.320 align:middle line:84%
And so you could imagine that
if people go into a learning

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.090 align:middle line:84%
environment, and they're like,
I'm not sure if this is for me.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:20.160 align:middle line:84%
Then you're paying attention to
not feeling like this is for me.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.160 align:middle line:84%
And you're not paying
attention to the learning.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:23.170 align:middle line:90%
And then a test comes along.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:24.650 align:middle line:84%
And you don't do as
well on that test.

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:26.690 align:middle line:84%
And then that makes you feel
like you belong even less.

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:28.560 align:middle line:84%
And then you're feeling
more unbelonging.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:29.810 align:middle line:90%
And then you don't do as well.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:31.160 align:middle line:90%
And you get a negative cycle.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.800 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine the
opposite, where you feel like,

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.760 align:middle line:84%
oh, I definitely belong in
this learning environment.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.500 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be a good
growth experience for me.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:37.930 align:middle line:84%
And then you are
focused on learning.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:38.810 align:middle line:90%
And then you do better.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:41.102 align:middle line:84%
And that makes you feel like
you belong, and on and on.

00:11:41.102 --> 00:11:44.350 align:middle line:84%
So we were really interested
in these entry points.

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:48.345 align:middle line:84%
And basically, every
class at Harvard and MIT

00:11:48.345 --> 00:11:50.470 align:middle line:84%
offered had a little
pre-course survey at the beg--

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:51.640 align:middle line:84%
have any of you ever
taken a class that

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:53.630 align:middle line:84%
has a little survey at the
beginning of it for your MOOCs?

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:55.005 align:middle line:84%
If you did it for
Harvard or MIT,

00:11:55.005 --> 00:12:00.790 align:middle line:84%
I probably wrote it, especially
if you did it a few years ago.

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:04.600 align:middle line:84%
And so we started
randomly assigning people

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.355 align:middle line:90%
these little interventions.

00:12:07.355 --> 00:12:08.980 align:middle line:84%
Like, you'd answer
a bunch of questions

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:10.480 align:middle line:84%
about what you know
about the topic.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.280 align:middle line:84%
And then we'd say
things like, write

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.800 align:middle line:90%
a letter to a future student--

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:16.540 align:middle line:90%
one was a values affirmation.

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.550 align:middle line:84%
Pick from this list
which of these values

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:19.900 align:middle line:90%
are really important to you.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:20.900 align:middle line:90%
And they-- I don't know.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:24.350 align:middle line:84%
They could be like loyalty,
personal growth, family,

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:25.660 align:middle line:90%
all kinds of things.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:29.800 align:middle line:84%
Write a short letter to yourself
about why taking this class

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:31.750 align:middle line:90%
aligns with those values.

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:35.350 align:middle line:84%
So asking people to just write
for a few minutes about why--

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:37.270 align:middle line:84%
we do other things
about belonging, right?

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:38.650 align:middle line:84%
Write a short letter
to a future student

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:41.180 align:middle line:84%
in this class about why they
should feel like they belong.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.530 align:middle line:90%
Things like that.

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:46.361 align:middle line:84%
And our hope was
is that it would

00:12:46.361 --> 00:12:48.880 align:middle line:84%
trigger this positive cascade
at the beginning of the class.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.130 align:middle line:90%
You feel like you belong more.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:51.120 align:middle line:90%
You feel like you value it more.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:51.870 align:middle line:90%
You do better.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:53.453 align:middle line:84%
You pay attention
more, you do better,

00:12:53.453 --> 00:12:55.280 align:middle line:84%
you learn more, all
those kinds of things.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:13:02.030 align:middle line:84%
We started by trying these
in just a couple of classes.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.910 align:middle line:84%
And even if we were picking
a couple of classes,

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:09.530 align:middle line:84%
we were still doing some of
the largest experiments that

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:11.810 align:middle line:84%
had ever been conducted
in higher education.

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:15.173 align:middle line:84%
We could pick a couple of
classes that might have 10,000

00:13:15.173 --> 00:13:17.090 align:middle line:84%
people that were actively
participating in it,

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:18.882 align:middle line:84%
and doing our things,
and things like that.

00:13:18.882 --> 00:13:19.880 align:middle line:90%
Kind of a lot of folks.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:22.160 align:middle line:84%
So here are four
things that we tried.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:26.030 align:middle line:84%
One was plan making,
having people write down

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.620 align:middle line:84%
exactly how they plan
to complete the class.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.522 align:middle line:84%
Like, when they were going to do
it, who was going to help them,

00:13:31.522 --> 00:13:33.480 align:middle line:84%
what they were going to
do when they get stuck?

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:34.520 align:middle line:90%
Those kinds of things.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:37.400 align:middle line:84%
This value relevance things
that you just described.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:40.240 align:middle line:84%
And then mental contrasting
with implementation impatient

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.220 align:middle line:84%
intentions is
basically like, what

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:45.080 align:middle line:84%
are the kinds of things
that might make you stuck?

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:48.730 align:middle line:84%
And how would you get unstuck
when you encounter these things?

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:52.700 align:middle line:84%
When we did them in one
or two classes at a time,

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:53.942 align:middle line:90%
they worked pretty well.

00:13:53.942 --> 00:13:56.150 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know how familiar
you are with statistics.

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:58.220 align:middle line:84%
But the beta parameters
are the one you look at.

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:00.970 align:middle line:84%
So this plan making things
people were four percentage

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:03.400 align:middle line:84%
points more likely to
finish the class if they got

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:05.350 align:middle line:84%
the intervention
than they didn't.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:06.640 align:middle line:90%
That's huge.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:09.642 align:middle line:84%
If you ask you ask people to
do something for five minutes

00:14:09.642 --> 00:14:11.350 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of
the class and they're

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.450 align:middle line:90%
4% more likely to finish it--

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.830 align:middle line:84%
just like the cost-benefit
of that is off the charts.

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:17.890 align:middle line:90%
That's fabulous.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:21.050 align:middle line:84%
This one was getting people
to do more activities,

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:22.460 align:middle line:84%
getting people to
persist longer,

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:24.167 align:middle line:84%
getting people to
complete better,

00:14:24.167 --> 00:14:25.250 align:middle line:90%
all these kinds of things.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:26.930 align:middle line:84%
And some of these
studies were huge--

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:30.310 align:middle line:84%
5,000 people, 3,000
people, 2000 people.

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.530 align:middle line:90%
That's pretty good.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:36.380 align:middle line:84%
We then were like, we were
pretty excited about ourselves.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:38.440 align:middle line:84%
We were like, we've
come up with some stuff

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:41.820 align:middle line:84%
that doesn't cost anything
and really helps learners.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.680 align:middle line:84%
Let's go ahead and
take these things

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:46.270 align:middle line:84%
and use them in
all of the classes.

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:51.240 align:middle line:84%
Let's let Harvard and MIT let
us test things in every class.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:56.220 align:middle line:84%
And it is not uncommon
in educational research,

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.660 align:middle line:84%
that if something works
in a little space,

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:02.070 align:middle line:84%
that when you try it at much
bigger scales, it doesn't work.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:04.523 align:middle line:84%
That is a very frustrating
finding, but also

00:15:04.523 --> 00:15:05.440 align:middle line:90%
a very common finding.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.470 align:middle line:84%
And that is exactly
what happened to us.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.850 align:middle line:84%
So we took these
things which, again,

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:12.370 align:middle line:84%
would have been considered
like huge studies in education.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:16.710 align:middle line:84%
Randomly assigning 4,000
people to a treatment or not is

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:17.650 align:middle line:90%
enormous.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.940 align:middle line:84%
And we did it with a
quarter of a million people,

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.560 align:middle line:90%
like a lot of learners.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:25.375 align:middle line:84%
And basically, everything
that we had tried and wrote

00:15:25.375 --> 00:15:27.000 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of papers
that said, hey, these

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.060 align:middle line:90%
are pretty good ideas--

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:29.670 align:middle line:84%
we had to be like,
actually, they

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:31.530 align:middle line:90%
don't really work that well.

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:39.130 align:middle line:84%
And part of the problem
was they appeared

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:42.200 align:middle line:84%
to work in some
classes, but not others.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.190 align:middle line:84%
And we couldn't make
any sense of why.

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:47.920 align:middle line:84%
Part of that is that it's hard
for us to know a whole lot--

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:49.750 align:middle line:84%
if we did it in
about 250 classes.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:51.250 align:middle line:84%
And it's hard to
know the difference

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:53.800 align:middle line:84%
between those different
kinds of things.

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:58.660 align:middle line:84%
But we listed characteristics
of all the classes.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.310 align:middle line:84%
And we put them into big AI
models to try to figure out why.

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:01.690 align:middle line:90%
And all these other kind--

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:03.280 align:middle line:90%
I mean, you can read the paper.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.600 align:middle line:90%
But it was an enormous effort.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.710 align:middle line:84%
And at the end of
it, a thing that we

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:10.210 align:middle line:84%
had lots of people
in there, but we

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:13.480 align:middle line:84%
weren't able to identify things
that worked particularly well.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:16.935 align:middle line:84%
And this was pretty consistent
across this enterprise.

00:16:16.935 --> 00:16:18.310 align:middle line:84%
In fact, to my
knowledge, there's

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:21.850 align:middle line:84%
virtually no one who's doing
research on Harvard or MIT--

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:24.610 align:middle line:84%
HarvardX or MITx
classes anymore.

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:29.475 align:middle line:84%
And so this was the
third of these big bets.

00:16:29.475 --> 00:16:32.290 align:middle line:90%


00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:34.150 align:middle line:84%
There are people
like Peter Norvig

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.550 align:middle line:84%
and who's a woman, Daphne
Koller, who formed Coursera, who

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.920 align:middle line:84%
thought that data-driven
education would

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.390 align:middle line:84%
be very similar to
data-driven medicine.

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:45.870 align:middle line:84%
Like, just like the
Human Genome Project

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:48.570 align:middle line:84%
revolutionized a data-driven
form of medicine,

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:52.350 align:middle line:84%
that Coursera, EdX could
revolutionize a data driven form

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.880 align:middle line:90%
of the science of learning.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.530 align:middle line:84%
And we knew a lot about
what people clicked,

00:16:56.530 --> 00:16:58.440 align:middle line:84%
but we never really
got much more data

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:01.620 align:middle line:84%
about what was going on
inside people's heads.

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:03.900 align:middle line:84%
The other huge issue
is that if you really

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:08.760 align:middle line:84%
want to do educational research,
that studies how to help people

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:13.082 align:middle line:84%
learn better, you need variation
in the course that's deeper

00:17:13.082 --> 00:17:14.290 align:middle line:90%
than what we were attempting.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:15.660 align:middle line:84%
You need to be able
to do more than just

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:17.140 align:middle line:84%
change a little thing at
the beginning of the course.

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:18.515 align:middle line:84%
You have to be
like, well, here's

00:17:18.515 --> 00:17:19.900 align:middle line:84%
one good way of
teaching a class.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.849 align:middle line:84%
Here's another good way
of teaching the class.

00:17:21.849 --> 00:17:23.619 align:middle line:90%
Let's try both of them.

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:26.220 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out that it was
very difficult to go to faculty

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:29.160 align:middle line:84%
and be like, will you make
two versions of this course,

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:32.320 align:middle line:84%
so that we can test some of
these different ideas in them--

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:34.590 align:middle line:84%
because people had barely
enough time and resources

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:36.795 align:middle line:84%
to make one version of
the course, and so forth.

00:17:36.795 --> 00:17:39.760 align:middle line:90%


00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:42.970 align:middle line:84%
So like the other two
big bets of MOOCs,

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:46.030 align:middle line:84%
they, for the most
part didn't pan out.

00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:49.180 align:middle line:84%
And I think, if you go
back 10 years from now

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:53.647 align:middle line:84%
and read the op-eds that David
Brooks and Thomas Friedman were

00:17:53.647 --> 00:17:56.230 align:middle line:84%
writing in The New York Times
about the totally transformative

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:59.440 align:middle line:84%
impact of online education,
people would be pretty

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:01.300 align:middle line:84%
surprised, 10 years
later, to find out

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:05.470 align:middle line:84%
that it's good for things
like executive education

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:07.518 align:middle line:84%
or helping people earn a
second master's degree,

00:18:07.518 --> 00:18:08.810 align:middle line:90%
which are not bad things to do.

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:13.810 align:middle line:84%
But again, they're
smaller components of it.

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.670 align:middle line:84%
I've thought a lot over the
last couple of years about, OK,

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:20.950 align:middle line:84%
so if we're not going to totally
reform the educational system

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:23.770 align:middle line:84%
with independent,
self-paced online learning,

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:25.780 align:middle line:90%
what else might we do?

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:29.750 align:middle line:84%
Well, so for instance, I've
told you that already affluent,

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:33.070 align:middle line:84%
already educated people are
reasonably likely to persist

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:34.170 align:middle line:90%
in online courses.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, there are all kinds
of already affluent, already

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:38.410 align:middle line:84%
educated people who work
in the public interest.

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:39.640 align:middle line:84%
There are people
who are teachers.

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:41.020 align:middle line:84%
There are people who
work in public health.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:42.520 align:middle line:84%
There are people
who work in nurses.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:44.490 align:middle line:84%
Lots of them work
in contexts where

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:46.260 align:middle line:84%
they don't have
a lot of learning

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:49.630 align:middle line:84%
after their college degree
or their advanced degree.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:52.170 align:middle line:84%
So what if we created
online courses

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:54.180 align:middle line:84%
that a good fraction
of people would be

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:56.250 align:middle line:90%
able to persist in and finish?

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:57.610 align:middle line:90%
That would be able to help them.

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.820 align:middle line:84%
There was one
course that we built

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:02.430 align:middle line:84%
called Launching Innovation
in schools, which

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:08.730 align:middle line:84%
relates to the stuff
in Iterate that you

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.560 align:middle line:90%
read a couple classes ago.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:12.465 align:middle line:84%
And in our course, we
make all of our-- not

00:19:12.465 --> 00:19:15.090 align:middle line:84%
everybody do this-- we make all
of our courses openly licensed.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:17.500 align:middle line:84%
So we make it so that
it's Creative Commons.

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:20.100 align:middle line:84%
Anybody can take our things
and do anything with them.

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:21.593 align:middle line:84%
There were some
folks in Ukraine,

00:19:21.593 --> 00:19:23.010 align:middle line:84%
before the war,
who had translated

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:24.960 align:middle line:84%
our courses into
Ukrainian and put them

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:26.820 align:middle line:90%
on a Ukrainian platform.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.750 align:middle line:84%
And for a while, at the
beginning of the war--

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:30.210 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if this is
still true-- teachers still

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:32.950 align:middle line:84%
had to get recertified, and do
training, and things like that.

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:36.840 align:middle line:84%
So some enormous fraction of
the Ukrainian teaching force

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:38.880 align:middle line:84%
had at least signed
up and plausibly

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.280 align:middle line:84%
taken one of these courses
and things like that.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.040 align:middle line:84%
So for us, it was of
a proof of concept

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:47.700 align:middle line:84%
that people can benefit from
some of these kinds of things.

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:53.640 align:middle line:84%
With Mariana's advisor, José,
we got really interested in what

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:55.110 align:middle line:84%
if the thing you
want is not really

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:59.370 align:middle line:84%
big MOOC providers, but very
small local MOOC providers.

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:00.960 align:middle line:84%
So there's an
organization called

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:05.190 align:middle line:84%
Bedrock in Jordan run by
the Queen Rania Foundation

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:08.430 align:middle line:84%
that makes MOOCs in Arabic for
people in the Arabic world.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:12.230 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of providers
that offer courses

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:13.980 align:middle line:84%
in the local language
of their environment

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:15.980 align:middle line:84%
that are tailored to the
particular environments

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:16.830 align:middle line:90%
that they're in.

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:21.810 align:middle line:84%
And these tables are unreadable,
except if you go in them

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:22.650 align:middle line:90%
and look at them.

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:25.770 align:middle line:84%
But there's evidence
that you could actually

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:28.560 align:middle line:84%
do a better job getting
people from more

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:31.860 align:middle line:84%
diverse educational
backgrounds if you offer them

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:38.022 align:middle line:84%
in local languages, with local
providers with the topic area

00:20:38.022 --> 00:20:40.230 align:middle line:84%
and content areas that would
be interesting to people

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:41.520 align:middle line:90%
in those local places.

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:48.180 align:middle line:84%
So what we needed was not like
giant, global, elite-driven

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:52.950 align:middle line:84%
online learning, but maybe
another pathway with lots

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:54.840 align:middle line:84%
of distributed local
online learning

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:58.530 align:middle line:84%
that was particularly tailored
to people's cultural interests,

00:20:58.530 --> 00:21:01.290 align:middle line:84%
and labor markets, and
civic spheres, and needs,

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:02.165 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:21:02.165 --> 00:21:06.270 align:middle line:90%


00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:09.630 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I always try to
remain open to the idea

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:12.210 align:middle line:84%
that any of these
technologies could

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:15.120 align:middle line:84%
have new people that
come along and refresh

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:16.680 align:middle line:90%
the ideas around them.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:20.130 align:middle line:84%
But we should probably
think of those claims

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:21.970 align:middle line:90%
with a pretty high bar.

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:23.490 align:middle line:84%
If someone comes
along, and says,

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:26.250 align:middle line:84%
they have a new AI-powered
MOOC platform or something like

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:26.955 align:middle line:90%
that--

00:21:26.955 --> 00:21:28.830 align:middle line:84%
now, you could be like,
well, let me tell you

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:32.610 align:middle line:84%
what the research suggests about
the original MOOC platform, what

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.968 align:middle line:84%
some of the limitations
were, what it accomplished,

00:21:34.968 --> 00:21:36.510 align:middle line:84%
why it tackled some
big problems that

00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:37.760 align:middle line:90%
turned out to be really hard--

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:41.880 align:middle line:90%


00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.840 align:middle line:84%
and then what is it that
a new effort would be

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.900 align:middle line:90%
able to build on top of that.

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:50.400 align:middle line:84%
Those are some of the things I
want to tell you about MOOCs.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:53.080 align:middle line:84%
What other things
struck you about them?

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:54.610 align:middle line:90%
Or, what questions did you have?

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:57.630 align:middle line:84%
Or, what have we discussed
in the last few classes

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:59.606 align:middle line:84%
that fit or don't fit
with your experiences?

00:21:59.606 --> 00:22:17.573 align:middle line:90%


00:22:17.573 --> 00:22:19.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, I guess just
a general question is like,

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:24.510 align:middle line:84%
what is your real motivation for
higher educational institutions

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:26.700 align:middle line:84%
to want MOOCs to
exist, considering

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:30.830 align:middle line:84%
that their entire business is
based off of them, not like--

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:32.140 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: It's weird right?

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:36.530 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] Yeah, like people
got super enthusiastic

00:22:36.530 --> 00:22:40.558 align:middle line:84%
for a long time about
digitizing faculty labor.

00:22:40.558 --> 00:22:42.350 align:middle line:84%
So what were some of
the things that people

00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:44.240 align:middle line:90%
would have said, at the time?

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:48.500 align:middle line:84%
In 2013, people would have said
something along the lines of--

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.640 align:middle line:84%
particularly for the
last 40 or 50 years,

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:54.020 align:middle line:84%
but maybe for 100-plus years,
there's a class of universities

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:57.800 align:middle line:84%
that brags every year about how
many students they don't serve.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:02.478 align:middle line:84%
Around this time every year,
Stanford and MIT and Harvard

00:23:02.478 --> 00:23:04.520 align:middle line:84%
will publish statistics
about how many applicants

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:06.020 align:middle line:90%
they rejected.

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:08.270 align:middle line:84%
But one of the things that
they could do with MOOCs

00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:11.330 align:middle line:84%
is start bragging about how
many people they served.

00:23:11.330 --> 00:23:14.550 align:middle line:84%
For the faculty, who
are especially early on,

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:17.520 align:middle line:84%
who got millions of people
to sign up for their courses,

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:18.950 align:middle line:90%
it was exhilarating.

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:21.350 align:middle line:84%
It was incredibly exciting
to be able to say--

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:23.210 align:middle line:84%
I mean, Naman
Aggarwal used to say,

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:29.360 align:middle line:84%
if I taught 6.11 at MIT
for the next 40 years,

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:32.070 align:middle line:84%
I still couldn't have as
many students who showed up

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:34.070 align:middle line:84%
in the first round of
this class that I offered,

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:34.945 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:23:34.945 --> 00:23:36.680 align:middle line:90%
So that was exciting.

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:41.480 align:middle line:84%
I have a hunch that
when people were afraid

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:44.820 align:middle line:84%
that this might profoundly
changed the education landscape,

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:47.570 align:middle line:84%
that elite universities
wanted to be in front of it,

00:23:47.570 --> 00:23:48.765 align:middle line:90%
rather than behind it.

00:23:48.765 --> 00:23:51.140 align:middle line:84%
Like, as soon as there was
some commercial offerings that

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:52.640 align:middle line:84%
were available,
there were folks who

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:58.377 align:middle line:84%
said, man, we really ought to
have a nonprofit alternative.

00:23:58.377 --> 00:24:00.710 align:middle line:84%
We ought to have universities
that are involved in this.

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:03.410 align:middle line:84%
I mean, another really
important structural factor

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:09.348 align:middle line:84%
is that university revenue
sources are constantly

00:24:09.348 --> 00:24:09.890 align:middle line:90%
under threat.

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:12.165 align:middle line:84%
And their costs are
constantly going up.

00:24:12.165 --> 00:24:14.040 align:middle line:84%
So health care costs
are constantly going up.

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:16.550 align:middle line:84%
Technology costs are
constantly going up.

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:18.470 align:middle line:84%
And if you're a
state university,

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:19.970 align:middle line:84%
like across the
United States, we've

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:23.480 align:middle line:84%
been dramatically reducing
the amount that the taxpaying

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:25.762 align:middle line:84%
public support schools
and asking students

00:24:25.762 --> 00:24:28.220 align:middle line:84%
to pay more tuition, and so
forth-- or, a higher percentage

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:29.840 align:middle line:90%
of their tuition.

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:32.390 align:middle line:84%
And so a bunch of folks
thought they could make money

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:34.520 align:middle line:84%
doing it-- not make money,
in the sense of putting

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:37.740 align:middle line:84%
their pocket-- but
make money doing that.

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:41.300 align:middle line:84%
So those are some of the
kinds of reasons that people

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:43.490 align:middle line:90%
got really excited about it.

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:49.295 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, in retrospect, in
some ways, you're like, why--

00:24:49.295 --> 00:24:51.920 align:middle line:84%
I mean, at the time, there were
definitely plenty of people who

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:54.950 align:middle line:84%
were like, you want to take this
thing that we sell to people

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:57.850 align:middle line:84%
for $80,000 a year and
give them to them for free?

00:24:57.850 --> 00:25:00.830 align:middle line:90%
Like, what kind of plan is that?

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:04.730 align:middle line:84%
Or, for 35 bucks or other
kinds of things like that.

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:06.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: One thing that
I was thinking about,

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:11.360 align:middle line:84%
with respect to presenting
books with like helping

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.310 align:middle line:84%
underserved
communities and stuff

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:18.210 align:middle line:90%
is like the issue of space.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:21.890 align:middle line:84%
Because to me, I
feel like having

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.300 align:middle line:84%
been in like a pretty
underserved high school

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:24.800 align:middle line:90%
and stuff--

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:27.620 align:middle line:84%
I was like, OK, well, even
like in a position of higher

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:32.720 align:middle line:84%
education, space is a very big
issue for a lot of these spaces.

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:35.490 align:middle line:84%
Where it's like, you simply
can't just sit at home.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:38.340 align:middle line:84%
And even if you have access to a
laptop or something to do that.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:41.000 align:middle line:84%
And it was like, I just
wanted to know if--

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:43.730 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this might
kind of also just

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:45.990 align:middle line:84%
connect to the larger
social aspect of it--

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:50.360 align:middle line:84%
but I was wondering
if this even came up

00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:54.380 align:middle line:84%
when they were talking about the
accessibility of these systems.

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:57.600 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I mean, I used to
joke that something like anyone,

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:01.520 align:middle line:84%
even the poorest peasant in the
most remote African village, who

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:04.070 align:middle line:84%
has high-speed internet and a
basic understanding of matrix

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:06.860 align:middle line:84%
algebra, can take the
Introduction Aeronautics class.

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:08.360 align:middle line:84%
And I was like,
yeah, there actually

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.830 align:middle line:84%
are not that many peasants, who
meet both of those criteria.

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:12.620 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:16.800 align:middle line:90%
But it was always the case.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:19.503 align:middle line:84%
There was a very famous case
of-- there was the article

00:26:19.503 --> 00:26:21.920 align:middle line:84%
in The New York Times about
him was called "The Boy Genius

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:23.490 align:middle line:90%
of Ulan Bator."

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:26.100 align:middle line:84%
So there was a student
who was in Mongolia,

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:29.100 align:middle line:84%
who got a perfect score
on the first MITx class

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:30.900 align:middle line:90%
and was admitted to MIT.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:34.770 align:middle line:84%
And this was taken as proof
that, like anybody, anywhere

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:37.270 align:middle line:84%
in the world can get
access to our courses.

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:39.788 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to be able
to find the best high school

00:26:39.788 --> 00:26:41.080 align:middle line:90%
students and bring them to MIT.

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:43.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, there were some
more details to the story.

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:45.930 align:middle line:84%
One is, the principal of
that school in Mongolia

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:47.770 align:middle line:84%
is, as I understood,
at the time,

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:50.363 align:middle line:84%
the only person from Mongolia
to have graduated from MIT.

00:26:50.363 --> 00:26:52.030 align:middle line:84%
So there was kind of
a connection there.

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:55.440 align:middle line:84%
Second, there was an
advanced physics class

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:57.360 align:middle line:84%
that was offered that
used the material.

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:58.860 align:middle line:84%
But there was a
postdoc, who I think

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:00.600 align:middle line:84%
was at Stanford or
some elite school

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:04.260 align:middle line:84%
in California, who went
and studied with these kids

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:05.940 align:middle line:90%
for a bunch of months.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:07.900 align:middle line:84%
So one thing you
could say is like, oh,

00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:10.090 align:middle line:84%
we just need to get
MOOCs into every school.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:11.770 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll help people
find their way to MIT.

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:12.780 align:middle line:84%
The other thing you can
say is like, we just

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:15.010 align:middle line:84%
need MIT grads being the
principal of every school.

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:15.510 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:17.290 align:middle line:84%
And then we can get
people coming to MIT.

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:18.520 align:middle line:84%
But this kid, his
name was Battushig.

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:20.530 align:middle line:84%
And he did a bunch of
education classes at MIT.

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:21.920 align:middle line:90%
He was a remarkable kid.

00:27:21.920 --> 00:27:23.960 align:middle line:84%
He used to watch two
videos at the same time

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:26.872 align:middle line:90%
when he was doing these classes.

00:27:26.872 --> 00:27:29.330 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't make any sense, but
there's a great news article

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:31.280 align:middle line:84%
that shows his little
setup of him watching

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:32.460 align:middle line:90%
two videos at the same time.

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:34.460 align:middle line:84%
I mean, he's
clearly a smart guy.

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:39.050 align:middle line:84%
But it was really important
to the narrative of MOOCs

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:40.650 align:middle line:90%
that, look--

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:42.590 align:middle line:84%
look at, look at how
far into the periphery

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:45.620 align:middle line:84%
that we've reached to
bring people into the core.

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:49.100 align:middle line:84%
And so folks like me, who
are writing papers, are like,

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:50.400 align:middle line:90%
that is neat.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:52.040 align:middle line:90%
That is a true story.

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:53.990 align:middle line:84%
Battushig is an
awesome kid that is not

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:59.750 align:middle line:84%
representative of the broader
system that we've built here.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:04.440 align:middle line:90%
And yeah, it was interesting.

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:06.500 align:middle line:84%
People really
wanted these things

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:08.720 align:middle line:90%
to be democratizing education.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:10.250 align:middle line:84%
But then, when it
was pretty obvious

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:12.660 align:middle line:84%
they weren't, they were
still like, yeah, well, OK.

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:14.510 align:middle line:90%
We'll just use them anyway.

00:28:14.510 --> 00:28:15.590 align:middle line:90%
I know.

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:17.880 align:middle line:90%
I helped support--

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:21.270 align:middle line:84%
I did an evaluation of the
Supply Chain Management program,

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:24.050 align:middle line:84%
which was one of the
first programs that said,

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:26.280 align:middle line:90%
do half the program online.

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:30.380 align:middle line:84%
So you have to take and pass
proctored tests in four or five

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:31.500 align:middle line:90%
online classes.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:33.535 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll give you
half a degree credit.

00:28:33.535 --> 00:28:34.910 align:middle line:84%
And you come to
campus and finish

00:28:34.910 --> 00:28:36.770 align:middle line:90%
the other half of the degree.

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:41.190 align:middle line:84%
And there are a lot of good
things about it, actually.

00:28:41.190 --> 00:28:42.920 align:middle line:84%
So one is supply
chain management

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:44.310 align:middle line:90%
is a relatively new field.

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:45.500 align:middle line:84%
And so there aren't
a lot of universities

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:46.710 align:middle line:90%
around the world that offer it.

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:47.918 align:middle line:90%
And MIT is pretty good at it.

00:28:47.918 --> 00:28:51.290 align:middle line:84%
So this was a good way of having
more people be able to do it.

00:28:51.290 --> 00:28:52.625 align:middle line:90%
It's a relatively small program.

00:28:52.625 --> 00:28:54.000 align:middle line:84%
They don't have
a ton of faculty.

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.593 align:middle line:84%
So in theory, you could
have twice as many people

00:28:56.593 --> 00:28:58.760 align:middle line:84%
come through campus if they
do half the stuff online

00:28:58.760 --> 00:28:59.880 align:middle line:90%
and half the stuff here.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.005 align:middle line:84%
And there were, actually,
some really cool findings

00:29:02.005 --> 00:29:03.920 align:middle line:90%
where the folks who--

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:06.980 align:middle line:84%
the way they did it is they
kept an in-person cohort

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:12.500 align:middle line:84%
and added an online cohort that
showed up with in-person cohort

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:13.910 align:middle line:90%
in January.

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:17.960 align:middle line:84%
And we did some surveys about
how both groups felt, in terms

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:19.163 align:middle line:90%
of their connection to MIT.

00:29:19.163 --> 00:29:21.330 align:middle line:84%
And they both felt really
strongly connected to MIT.

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:24.210 align:middle line:84%
Actually, in sort of funny ways,
like on campus people were like,

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:25.260 align:middle line:90%
we've been here all year.

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:26.260 align:middle line:90%
Like, this is our place.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:28.927 align:middle line:84%
And the online people were like,
we've been taking these classes

00:29:28.927 --> 00:29:30.180 align:middle line:90%
together for three years.

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:31.350 align:middle line:90%
Like, this is our place.

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:32.767 align:middle line:84%
So they both felt
really connected

00:29:32.767 --> 00:29:34.010 align:middle line:90%
in interesting kind of ways.

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:36.650 align:middle line:84%
The grades of the online folks
were actually slightly better

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:37.980 align:middle line:90%
at the end of the first cohort.

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:41.040 align:middle line:84%
Although, who knows if that
would have persisted into time.

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:43.970 align:middle line:84%
But the only people
who persisted

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:47.000 align:middle line:84%
through the online courses
were people who already

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:48.500 align:middle line:84%
had the jobs in
logistics industries

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.833 align:middle line:84%
were getting their second
degree, were already affluent.

00:29:50.833 --> 00:29:52.280 align:middle line:84%
They were
disproportionately guys.

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:55.700 align:middle line:84%
There's a whole line
of research that

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:59.570 align:middle line:84%
really focuses on
people's ability

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:01.170 align:middle line:84%
to conduct
self-regulated learning,

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:03.980 align:middle line:84%
people's ability to
persist through difficulty,

00:30:03.980 --> 00:30:05.183 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:30:05.183 --> 00:30:06.350 align:middle line:90%
And so you talk to learners.

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:09.230 align:middle line:84%
And they would talk about how
they took their lunch breaks

00:30:09.230 --> 00:30:11.540 align:middle line:84%
and they studied, how they
took weekends and studied,

00:30:11.540 --> 00:30:13.623 align:middle line:84%
how they stayed up late
at night and they studied.

00:30:13.623 --> 00:30:15.740 align:middle line:84%
And especially if
you think about it

00:30:15.740 --> 00:30:18.570 align:middle line:84%
through an individual
psychological perspective,

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:21.380 align:middle line:84%
you're like, yeah, those
are all great qualities.

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:23.130 align:middle line:84%
Another thing we
realized, though,

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:27.150 align:middle line:84%
is like if you're
studying in the evenings,

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:30.032 align:middle line:84%
you're probably shirking
family responsibilities.

00:30:30.032 --> 00:30:31.740 align:middle line:84%
And in lots of cultures
across the world,

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.250 align:middle line:84%
it is way easier for guys to
shirk family responsibilities

00:30:34.250 --> 00:30:36.500 align:middle line:84%
than it is for women to shirk
family responsibilities.

00:30:36.500 --> 00:30:38.390 align:middle line:84%
So in some ways,
self-regulated learning

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:40.830 align:middle line:84%
was the same thing as shirking
family responsibility.

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:43.100 align:middle line:84%
Like a thing that you'd study
from a psychological perspective

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:45.225 align:middle line:84%
is self-regulated learning
would be shirking family

00:30:45.225 --> 00:30:47.870 align:middle line:84%
responsibilities, from a
sociological point of view.

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:52.130 align:middle line:84%
And so, again, just giving us
more ways to think about, as we

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:55.088 align:middle line:84%
create learning environments,
who will benefit from them?

00:30:55.088 --> 00:30:57.380 align:middle line:84%
What are the enabling conditions
to help lots of people

00:30:57.380 --> 00:30:59.420 align:middle line:84%
from lots of different kinds
of backgrounds and places

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:01.087 align:middle line:84%
in society be able
to benefit from them?

00:31:01.087 --> 00:31:03.240 align:middle line:90%
Things like that.

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:05.240 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think something
that stood out to me

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:07.260 align:middle line:84%
was the business
model of the MOOCs.

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:09.590 align:middle line:84%
I think I don't
really understand it

00:31:09.590 --> 00:31:11.390 align:middle line:84%
because, I think,
for me, as someone

00:31:11.390 --> 00:31:14.355 align:middle line:84%
who went to international school
in Africa-- and I would say,

00:31:14.355 --> 00:31:17.450 align:middle line:84%
I was what, maybe like top five,
like in my country or something.

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:19.280 align:middle line:90%
And pretty big, like in Africa.

00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:22.070 align:middle line:84%
Like I'd never heard of
any MIT or Harvard course,

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:22.750 align:middle line:90%
like in my life.

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:25.250 align:middle line:84%
I'd probably say, none of my
teachers have ever heard of it.

00:31:25.250 --> 00:31:29.750 align:middle line:84%
And I think it made me wonder,
who exactly they're targeting.

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:31.480 align:middle line:84%
Because I don't think
it's necessarily

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:34.790 align:middle line:84%
young high schoolers, like
trying to get into college.

00:31:34.790 --> 00:31:37.160 align:middle line:84%
Because what I did realize
when I came to MIT,

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:39.370 align:middle line:84%
and when I Googled like
how to do linear algebra,

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:41.590 align:middle line:84%
or all these things
is then I would

00:31:41.590 --> 00:31:44.050 align:middle line:84%
see MIT courses, and
MIT OpenCourseWare,

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:46.210 align:middle line:84%
and all these things
pop up on YouTube.

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:47.710 align:middle line:84%
But then, back in
high school, and I

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:49.100 align:middle line:84%
didn't know what
linear algebra is--

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:50.480 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: If you don't
know what linear algebra was,

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:51.913 align:middle line:84%
how are you going to search
for a linear algebra course---

00:31:51.913 --> 00:31:52.160 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it was
something that came to my mind--

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:52.640 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, yeah.

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:54.290 align:middle line:90%
No, that's a terrific example.

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:56.030 align:middle line:90%
I mean, the business model--

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:57.760 align:middle line:84%
I mean Daphne
Koller, and I think,

00:31:57.760 --> 00:32:00.070 align:middle line:84%
in one of her first talks,
refers to it as a blue ocean

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:01.810 align:middle line:84%
business model,
which is, basically,

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:05.507 align:middle line:84%
the potential addressable market
is like six billion people

00:32:05.507 --> 00:32:06.590 align:middle line:90%
in the world or something.

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:09.040 align:middle line:84%
Anybody who's got an
internet connection,

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:12.730 align:middle line:84%
if you can sell $35
certificates a couple of times

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:14.480 align:middle line:84%
to six billion people
over and over again,

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:18.142 align:middle line:84%
you can make a lot
of money doing that.

00:32:18.142 --> 00:32:21.700 align:middle line:84%
And there were efforts
to try to create

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:23.030 align:middle line:90%
institutional connections.

00:32:23.030 --> 00:32:25.850 align:middle line:84%
So CS50, who's Harvard's
computer science class,

00:32:25.850 --> 00:32:29.210 align:middle line:84%
has a whole program
to help high schools

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:31.630 align:middle line:84%
to adopt the online
CS50 materials

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:33.880 align:middle line:90%
in their in-person instruction.

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:37.263 align:middle line:84%
So there's some fraction that
you, hanging out in Africa,

00:32:37.263 --> 00:32:38.680 align:middle line:84%
would have encountered
this course

00:32:38.680 --> 00:32:40.840 align:middle line:90%
and just decided to take it.

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:43.090 align:middle line:84%
There's some sense
that your school

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:45.342 align:middle line:84%
might have made--
connected with one

00:32:45.342 --> 00:32:46.550 align:middle line:90%
of these formal institutions.

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:49.150 align:middle line:84%
There was another great
story of the guy who founded

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.870 align:middle line:90%
stripe was from St. Louis.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:55.550 align:middle line:84%
And was like, I want
to live in St. Louis.

00:32:55.550 --> 00:32:57.500 align:middle line:84%
And I can't hire any
developers in St. Louis.

00:32:57.500 --> 00:32:58.280 align:middle line:90%
There aren't enough developers.

00:32:58.280 --> 00:32:59.090 align:middle line:90%
I'm pretty sure it was St Louis.

00:32:59.090 --> 00:33:00.257 align:middle line:90%
I might have the town wrong.

00:33:00.257 --> 00:33:04.240 align:middle line:84%
So when MOOCs came
out, he was like, oh,

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:06.770 align:middle line:90%
let's offer CS50 in St. Louis.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:09.020 align:middle line:84%
And we'll start like building
infrastructure for this.

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:11.380 align:middle line:84%
And they started
some pilot program.

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:14.140 align:middle line:84%
And it completely
filled the library.

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:16.540 align:middle line:84%
There's hundreds of people,
who with a little additional

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:18.790 align:middle line:84%
support-- so they ended up
they ended up like creating

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:20.120 align:middle line:90%
a community space for it.

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:21.800 align:middle line:90%
So you use the online materials.

00:33:21.800 --> 00:33:23.710 align:middle line:84%
That was-- I think
there were models

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:26.980 align:middle line:84%
like that to try to bridge the
gap that you're describing.

00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:31.270 align:middle line:84%
That it's not realistic
to just have kids

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:32.740 align:middle line:84%
encounter these
classes and decided

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:34.740 align:middle line:84%
to get excited about them,
and things like that.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:38.680 align:middle line:84%
Still, almost all of those
intermediate bridging layers

00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:41.000 align:middle line:90%
require human capital.

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.590 align:middle line:90%
And humans are super expensive.

00:33:42.590 --> 00:33:47.590 align:middle line:84%
And then you erase the kind
of financial advantages

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:49.640 align:middle line:84%
that the MOOCs were
promoting in the beginning.

00:33:49.640 --> 00:33:51.730 align:middle line:84%
Lots of online programs
have found this.

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:54.190 align:middle line:84%
It's pretty cheap to
offer online classes,

00:33:54.190 --> 00:33:56.450 align:middle line:84%
but a lot of times people
just drop out of them.

00:33:56.450 --> 00:33:58.158 align:middle line:84%
If you want them to
not drop out of them,

00:33:58.158 --> 00:33:59.860 align:middle line:84%
you have to hire
coaches, and advisors,

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:02.210 align:middle line:84%
and assistant instructors,
and things like that.

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:04.720 align:middle line:84%
And then you've just-- you
brought back all the costs

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:06.922 align:middle line:84%
that you got rid
of from teaching.

00:34:06.922 --> 00:34:08.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
like a statistic

00:34:08.380 --> 00:34:11.800 align:middle line:84%
on how often state
universities create MOOCs

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:16.489 align:middle line:90%
versus these Ivy-esque schools?

00:34:16.489 --> 00:34:19.690 align:middle line:84%
Because the thought occurs to
me that maybe the reason why

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:22.870 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't matter if Harvard
or MIT or Stanford puts out

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:24.730 align:middle line:84%
a MOOC is because
you're not coming

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:27.328 align:middle line:84%
to Harvard or MIT or Stanford
just for the information.

00:34:27.328 --> 00:34:28.120 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:34:28.120 --> 00:34:29.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because,
obviously, what's

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:33.820 align:middle line:84%
associated with that is kind of
like this title and this class

00:34:33.820 --> 00:34:37.219 align:middle line:84%
stratification thing that isn't
accessible through a MOOC.

00:34:37.219 --> 00:34:40.179 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't matter if
the entire world has access

00:34:40.179 --> 00:34:43.179 align:middle line:84%
to a course that's meant for
learning-- because part of what

00:34:43.179 --> 00:34:45.760 align:middle line:84%
you pay for and part
of the real target

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:47.830 align:middle line:84%
audience of these
universities is people

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:49.389 align:middle line:90%
who want the title associated.

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:51.639 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Well, there
was an economist at Stanford

00:34:51.639 --> 00:34:53.530 align:middle line:84%
who wrote a cool
paper that says,

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:55.989 align:middle line:84%
the way you have to understand
elite education funding

00:34:55.989 --> 00:35:00.610 align:middle line:84%
models is that they roughly
offer their education for free,

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:04.270 align:middle line:84%
in the hopes of having a very
small percentage of people

00:35:04.270 --> 00:35:07.480 align:middle line:84%
pay very large amounts of money
for their education later.

00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:13.485 align:middle line:84%
Basically, we subsidize people's
tuition in all kinds of ways.

00:35:13.485 --> 00:35:15.110 align:middle line:84%
Even if your tuition
feels really high,

00:35:15.110 --> 00:35:17.920 align:middle line:84%
your tuition is subsidized
far beyond what it actually

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:19.040 align:middle line:90%
costs to educate you.

00:35:19.040 --> 00:35:21.820 align:middle line:84%
And then, hopefully, 1% of
you will give tens of millions

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:23.900 align:middle line:90%
of dollars back to MIT later.

00:35:23.900 --> 00:35:28.210 align:middle line:84%
And so this person was trying
to think about like given that's

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:31.870 align:middle line:84%
actually how elite
universities are funded,

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:34.610 align:middle line:84%
what effect will MOOCs have on
those kinds of relationships?

00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:37.960 align:middle line:84%
But there were plenty
of public institutions.

00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:40.210 align:middle line:84%
One reason that public
institutions got really excited

00:35:40.210 --> 00:35:42.040 align:middle line:84%
about it is their
job is to serve

00:35:42.040 --> 00:35:44.020 align:middle line:84%
as many people in the
state as possible.

00:35:44.020 --> 00:35:47.230 align:middle line:84%
And if you're in a
geographically large state,

00:35:47.230 --> 00:35:49.630 align:middle line:84%
like Virginia, and you're
the University of Virginia

00:35:49.630 --> 00:35:52.005 align:middle line:84%
in the middle of it, like it's
pretty hard to help people

00:35:52.005 --> 00:35:54.940 align:middle line:84%
in the Tidewater, and
in the southwest area,

00:35:54.940 --> 00:35:57.580 align:middle line:84%
and in the DC area, and
other things like that.

00:35:57.580 --> 00:36:00.020 align:middle line:84%
So Michigan made
big investments.

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:01.570 align:middle line:90%
Texas made big investments.

00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:05.710 align:middle line:84%
There were lots of
flagship public schools.

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:08.352 align:middle line:84%
Probably the most
distinctive one,

00:36:08.352 --> 00:36:10.810 align:middle line:84%
maybe not in MOOCs, but just
in online learning, generally,

00:36:10.810 --> 00:36:13.300 align:middle line:84%
is Arizona State
University, which has really

00:36:13.300 --> 00:36:15.310 align:middle line:84%
transformed itself
through online learning

00:36:15.310 --> 00:36:18.010 align:middle line:84%
into a global institution--
which they really

00:36:18.010 --> 00:36:19.210 align:middle line:90%
see as mission-driven.

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:22.378 align:middle line:84%
Like how do we just have as many
people as possible in Arizona

00:36:22.378 --> 00:36:24.670 align:middle line:84%
beyond Arizona get access to
the learning opportunities

00:36:24.670 --> 00:36:25.870 align:middle line:90%
we create?

00:36:25.870 --> 00:36:29.060 align:middle line:84%
And there's something very
noble that I find about that.

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:31.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I wanted to follow
up on your first question

00:36:31.870 --> 00:36:32.960 align:middle line:90%
about the business model.

00:36:32.960 --> 00:36:36.850 align:middle line:84%
How have for-profit
universities reacted to MOOCs,

00:36:36.850 --> 00:36:40.480 align:middle line:84%
since their entire
model is trying

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:43.030 align:middle line:84%
to serve as many underserved
people as possible

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:45.230 align:middle line:84%
and just like
charging them a lot?

00:36:45.230 --> 00:36:48.560 align:middle line:84%
And the fact is that a lot of
them are also online on the--

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:49.510 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:36:49.510 --> 00:36:50.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:52.313 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like the University
of Phoenix, I think.

00:36:52.313 --> 00:36:55.930 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, University
of Phoenix, Kaplan, Southern New

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:58.580 align:middle line:84%
Hampshire University,
Western Governors University.

00:36:58.580 --> 00:37:01.390 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think there are
some Southern New Hampshire

00:37:01.390 --> 00:37:04.690 align:middle line:84%
University would have
really welcomed--

00:37:04.690 --> 00:37:07.940 align:middle line:84%
In some way, some of their
reactions were like, hey team,

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:08.930 align:middle line:90%
this is not new.

00:37:08.930 --> 00:37:11.240 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly what we've
been doing for decades.

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:13.810 align:middle line:84%
So a weird thing was
that there was a status

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:14.980 align:middle line:90%
flip in online learning.

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:19.517 align:middle line:84%
In 2010, online learning was
a very low-status activity.

00:37:19.517 --> 00:37:21.100 align:middle line:84%
It would have been
primarily conducted

00:37:21.100 --> 00:37:22.900 align:middle line:84%
at community colleges,
for-profit colleges, state

00:37:22.900 --> 00:37:23.540 align:middle line:90%
colleges.

00:37:23.540 --> 00:37:27.550 align:middle line:84%
And overnight, instantly,
Stanford, MIT, and Harvard

00:37:27.550 --> 00:37:29.260 align:middle line:90%
made it an elite activity.

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:31.390 align:middle line:84%
They instantly--
there's actually

00:37:31.390 --> 00:37:33.400 align:middle line:84%
all kinds of sociological
theory about this,

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:37.330 align:middle line:84%
where the middle
class people have

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:39.720 align:middle line:84%
to avoid the activities
of lower class people

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:41.720 align:middle line:84%
and pursue the activities
of upper class people.

00:37:41.720 --> 00:37:42.970 align:middle line:84%
And lower class people
pursue the activity

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:43.730 align:middle line:90%
of upper class people.

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:45.188 align:middle line:84%
But upper class
people can actually

00:37:45.188 --> 00:37:47.140 align:middle line:84%
pursue the activities
of lower class people

00:37:47.140 --> 00:37:48.440 align:middle line:90%
because their status is safe.

00:37:48.440 --> 00:37:49.590 align:middle line:84%
Like, every once
in a while, there

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:51.160 align:middle line:84%
are trends come
along of people who

00:37:51.160 --> 00:37:59.370 align:middle line:84%
dress like trash or other
kinds of adopting lower class

00:37:59.370 --> 00:37:59.870 align:middle line:90%
activities.

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:01.328 align:middle line:84%
So there's some
sociological theory

00:38:01.328 --> 00:38:03.910 align:middle line:90%
that is behind some of that.

00:38:03.910 --> 00:38:06.370 align:middle line:84%
But it was one of
the things that

00:38:06.370 --> 00:38:08.350 align:middle line:90%
was striking about that moment.

00:38:08.350 --> 00:38:10.850 align:middle line:84%
So a bunch of these universities
were like, give me a break.

00:38:10.850 --> 00:38:11.570 align:middle line:90%
We've been doing this forever.

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:13.248 align:middle line:84%
I gave a talk at
Oakland University,

00:38:13.248 --> 00:38:15.290 align:middle line:84%
which is kind of a community
college in Michigan.

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:20.050 align:middle line:84%
And they were like, they'd be
like, why are you using video?

00:38:20.050 --> 00:38:22.077 align:middle line:90%
Like text is so much better.

00:38:22.077 --> 00:38:23.410 align:middle line:90%
We've been doing this for years.

00:38:23.410 --> 00:38:25.700 align:middle line:84%
Like, lots of people
can't download the video.

00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:27.290 align:middle line:90%
Like bandwidth is not that good.

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:30.040 align:middle line:84%
And there's not really a
benefit to watching someone

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:32.290 align:middle line:84%
say something versus
reading what they would say.

00:38:32.290 --> 00:38:33.530 align:middle line:90%
Like, what are you all doing?

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:35.280 align:middle line:84%
And be like, you might
have a point there.

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:36.040 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:38.230 align:middle line:84%
Because they were doing
something exciting.

00:38:38.230 --> 00:38:41.200 align:middle line:84%
But on the other hand,
if you're really fired up

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:44.122 align:middle line:84%
about online learning, and
Harvard, and MIT, and Stanford,

00:38:44.122 --> 00:38:45.830 align:middle line:84%
and Johns Hopkins,
and Michigan are like,

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:47.980 align:middle line:84%
no, online learning
is cool now--

00:38:47.980 --> 00:38:50.530 align:middle line:84%
there's ways that you
can use that to raise

00:38:50.530 --> 00:38:52.420 align:middle line:84%
your status, to legitimize
your activities,

00:38:52.420 --> 00:38:53.630 align:middle line:90%
and other things like that.

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:54.645 align:middle line:90%
So existing players.

00:38:54.645 --> 00:38:57.670 align:middle line:90%


00:38:57.670 --> 00:39:02.710 align:middle line:84%
And they weren't really pursuing
similar kinds of markets,

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:03.830 align:middle line:90%
for the most part.

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:06.340 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are a
handful of MOOC efforts

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:09.440 align:middle line:84%
that tried to help people
earn bachelor's degrees.

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:12.100 align:middle line:84%
And they were mostly
totally unsuccessful.

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:14.620 align:middle line:84%
And there weren't even really
that many of them that were

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:17.230 align:middle line:84%
tried, which that is kind of
like the sweet spot of most

00:39:17.230 --> 00:39:20.860 align:middle line:90%
for-profit colleges--

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:23.140 align:middle line:84%
which in many respects,
there's a lot of evidence

00:39:23.140 --> 00:39:25.420 align:middle line:84%
that they're just
a disaster for--

00:39:25.420 --> 00:39:26.840 align:middle line:90%
a lot of people don't finish.

00:39:26.840 --> 00:39:28.870 align:middle line:84%
People finish with
a lot of debt.

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:31.450 align:middle line:84%
Tressie McMillan Cottom wrote
a book called Lower Ed, where

00:39:31.450 --> 00:39:34.750 align:middle line:84%
she described them as a negative
social insurance program, where

00:39:34.750 --> 00:39:36.820 align:middle line:84%
on average, people are
worse after finding

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:38.778 align:middle line:84%
their ways into them than
they were beforehand.

00:39:38.778 --> 00:39:40.950 align:middle line:84%
Her book, Lower Ed
is pretty fabulous.

00:39:40.950 --> 00:39:41.450 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:42.950 align:middle line:84%
Let's make sure
that we get a chance

00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:46.600 align:middle line:84%
to talk a little bit about part
two, which is Algorithm-Guided

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:47.690 align:middle line:90%
Learning At Scale.

00:39:47.690 --> 00:39:50.560 align:middle line:84%
So it sounds like
a bunch of you--

00:39:50.560 --> 00:39:53.090 align:middle line:84%
we're heading into new territory
for me, which is like--

00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:57.470 align:middle line:84%
well, raise your hand if
in your K-12 experience,

00:39:57.470 --> 00:39:59.890 align:middle line:84%
you did something that you
think was algorithm-guided

00:39:59.890 --> 00:40:00.710 align:middle line:90%
learning at scale.

00:40:00.710 --> 00:40:01.670 align:middle line:84%
Like you sat in
front of a thing--

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:02.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:04.890 align:middle line:84%
Like this, by far, the highest
percentage that I've had

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:07.200 align:middle line:90%
in this class, thus far.

00:40:07.200 --> 00:40:12.305 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know, five years
ago, like a handful of people

00:40:12.305 --> 00:40:13.680 align:middle line:84%
wouldn't have done
it or had done

00:40:13.680 --> 00:40:14.883 align:middle line:90%
a little bit as a curiosity.

00:40:14.883 --> 00:40:16.800 align:middle line:84%
But like, clearly, if
you all know what IXL is

00:40:16.800 --> 00:40:20.010 align:middle line:84%
and things like that, this is
becoming a much more mainstream

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:25.080 align:middle line:90%
activity in people's classes.

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:27.112 align:middle line:90%
What was your sense of them?

00:40:27.112 --> 00:40:29.320 align:middle line:84%
So Dana's sense was that
they were kind of pointless.

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:31.320 align:middle line:84%
And she could just have
her mom do them for her.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:32.790 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:32.790 --> 00:40:36.670 align:middle line:84%
What were other people's
sentiments about them?

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:38.310 align:middle line:90%
Emily.

00:40:38.310 --> 00:40:40.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, for me,
like in high school,

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:42.300 align:middle line:84%
we used this thing
called Moonbeam, where

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:44.100 align:middle line:84%
it was like, you would
take an assessment

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:46.600 align:middle line:84%
and it would kind of like rank
you like where you're reading

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:48.070 align:middle line:90%
and your vocab skills are at.

00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:52.440 align:middle line:84%
And then you log in every
week to do it, and learn,

00:40:52.440 --> 00:40:53.620 align:middle line:90%
and progress, I guess.

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:57.240 align:middle line:84%
And I thought it
was useful for us

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:00.000 align:middle line:84%
because, even though we
had like regular classes

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:03.910 align:middle line:84%
or honors classes for English,
like within that class,

00:41:03.910 --> 00:41:05.198 align:middle line:90%
like everybody's vocab like--

00:41:05.198 --> 00:41:06.490 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: It was different.

00:41:06.490 --> 00:41:07.573 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It varied a lot.

00:41:07.573 --> 00:41:10.290 align:middle line:84%
And it was very hard
for the teachers

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:12.970 align:middle line:84%
to figure out what
kind of vocab to teach,

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:15.555 align:middle line:84%
what materials to provide
for us because everybody

00:41:15.555 --> 00:41:16.430 align:middle line:90%
had different things.

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:16.570 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:17.340 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And like
a lot of times,

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:19.180 align:middle line:84%
we would just sit in class,
like, I already know this word.

00:41:19.180 --> 00:41:19.650 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH:
Yeah, this is dumb.

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:20.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And like, you
just stop paying attention.

00:41:20.950 --> 00:41:22.497 align:middle line:84%
But then other kids
are like, well,

00:41:22.497 --> 00:41:23.830 align:middle line:90%
I have never heard of this word.

00:41:23.830 --> 00:41:24.790 align:middle line:90%
I need to learn it.

00:41:24.790 --> 00:41:24.990 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:25.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:41:25.490 --> 00:41:26.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: For us it was cool.

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:30.900 align:middle line:84%
But then, one of my
friend, she assessed it.

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:36.040 align:middle line:84%
And she started off in
the highest level ranking.

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:39.240 align:middle line:84%
And then after she finished,
they just bumped her back down

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:40.510 align:middle line:90%
to level one.

00:41:40.510 --> 00:41:44.700 align:middle line:84%
So then she just went back
to learning like blue,

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:47.073 align:middle line:84%
green in like high school
and sophomore year.

00:41:47.073 --> 00:41:47.740 align:middle line:90%
So it was like--

00:41:47.740 --> 00:41:49.450 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: That sounds
like a flawed system.

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:49.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:41:49.770 --> 00:41:51.353 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I will
tell you, though,

00:41:51.353 --> 00:41:53.250 align:middle line:84%
when I was in the
fourth grade, we

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:54.870 align:middle line:90%
had a similar version of that.

00:41:54.870 --> 00:41:56.620 align:middle line:84%
They were called
SRI reading cards.

00:41:56.620 --> 00:42:01.020 align:middle line:84%
It was like a file box, and it
was a bunch of short stories

00:42:01.020 --> 00:42:04.270 align:middle line:84%
or nonfiction passages that were
arranged in order of difficulty.

00:42:04.270 --> 00:42:07.040 align:middle line:84%
And you would just go
and read one and answer

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:08.290 align:middle line:90%
a bunch of questions about it.

00:42:08.290 --> 00:42:09.570 align:middle line:84%
And if you got them
all right, you'd

00:42:09.570 --> 00:42:11.237 align:middle line:84%
go on to the next one
or a couple later.

00:42:11.237 --> 00:42:12.930 align:middle line:84%
And if you got them
less, well, then you

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:15.180 align:middle line:84%
went back to earlier ones,
or your teacher helped you,

00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:17.150 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:42:17.150 --> 00:42:19.650 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, it's something that
will come up, but this notion

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:21.150 align:middle line:84%
that like, oh,
what would it look

00:42:21.150 --> 00:42:24.030 align:middle line:84%
like to figure out what kinds
of learning experiences people

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:26.490 align:middle line:84%
need, in that moment, and try
to meet that need, instead

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:29.280 align:middle line:84%
of teaching everybody the
same thing at the same time,

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:30.910 align:middle line:84%
has been compelling
for a long time.

00:42:30.910 --> 00:42:32.618 align:middle line:84%
It sounds like it was
compelling for you,

00:42:32.618 --> 00:42:34.170 align:middle line:84%
at least until the
system freaked out

00:42:34.170 --> 00:42:36.700 align:middle line:84%
and made you learn
kindergarten words again.

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:39.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, we had something
that like reading as well.

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:41.040 align:middle line:84%
Well, I think one
of the issues was

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:45.310 align:middle line:84%
it was not nearly as
consistent because I mean,

00:42:45.310 --> 00:42:47.340 align:middle line:84%
it was meant to be
done once a week

00:42:47.340 --> 00:42:49.990 align:middle line:84%
or twice a week, like a lot of
the examples we saw in class.

00:42:49.990 --> 00:42:51.900 align:middle line:84%
But it'd be like,
oh, like whenever

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:55.725 align:middle line:84%
the teacher didn't want to
deal with us to kind of bring

00:42:55.725 --> 00:42:56.560 align:middle line:90%
in the cards.

00:42:56.560 --> 00:42:57.660 align:middle line:90%
It's like, OK, do this.

00:42:57.660 --> 00:42:59.190 align:middle line:90%
So it was so inconsistent.

00:42:59.190 --> 00:43:02.130 align:middle line:84%
That I was like, OK,
well, I don't exactly

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:04.440 align:middle line:84%
know if I'm improving
or I'm staying stagnant

00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:06.690 align:middle line:84%
because I haven't seen this
program for two weeks now.

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:09.273 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Good, you've said
two things there that I really

00:43:09.273 --> 00:43:11.070 align:middle line:90%
want you to hang on to.

00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:14.520 align:middle line:84%
One is you said something which
Larry Cuban would have said

00:43:14.520 --> 00:43:16.380 align:middle line:84%
about television
things or things

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:18.870 align:middle line:84%
like that, which is that the
designers of this program

00:43:18.870 --> 00:43:21.453 align:middle line:84%
have a vision, but the teachers
have a different set of needs.

00:43:21.453 --> 00:43:24.037 align:middle line:84%
So the teachers are like, I need
to do some grading today or I

00:43:24.037 --> 00:43:26.280 align:middle line:84%
don't feel well, we're going
to do this thing-- which

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:28.905 align:middle line:84%
would have been the exact same
thing as rolling into television

00:43:28.905 --> 00:43:30.900 align:middle line:84%
and putting on a movie
for people to watch.

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:34.080 align:middle line:84%
And also, that the
systems have some kind

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:37.590 align:middle line:84%
of pedagogical model behind
them and their effectiveness

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:41.310 align:middle line:84%
may have to do with how well
that pedagogical model is

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:43.560 align:middle line:90%
implemented.

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:45.570 align:middle line:84%
So if the system
is designed to be

00:43:45.570 --> 00:43:49.140 align:middle line:84%
used with some consistency,
twice a week in six week chunks

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.660 align:middle line:84%
or something like that--
and you don't use it

00:43:51.660 --> 00:43:54.973 align:middle line:84%
with that program
design in mind--

00:43:54.973 --> 00:43:56.890 align:middle line:84%
it might not accomplish
what you wanted to do.

00:43:56.890 --> 00:43:59.580 align:middle line:84%
So it's not just a matter of
like downloading the software

00:43:59.580 --> 00:44:01.050 align:middle line:90%
onto kids' computers.

00:44:01.050 --> 00:44:03.482 align:middle line:84%
It's a matter of getting whole
systems to implement them.

00:44:03.482 --> 00:44:05.190 align:middle line:84%
We're going to look
at some research I'll

00:44:05.190 --> 00:44:06.215 align:middle line:90%
show you on Monday.

00:44:06.215 --> 00:44:07.840 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if I'll
make you read it--

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:09.660 align:middle line:84%
but I'll just
summarize it for you--

00:44:09.660 --> 00:44:11.460 align:middle line:84%
about some of these
intelligent tutors

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:15.270 align:middle line:84%
that are really focused
on letting people

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:18.070 align:middle line:84%
work on problems that
are challenging for them,

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:20.310 align:middle line:84%
even if it's from material
that is from earlier

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:21.990 align:middle line:90%
in the year or previous years.

00:44:21.990 --> 00:44:25.230 align:middle line:84%
And that system only
works if teachers actually

00:44:25.230 --> 00:44:26.850 align:middle line:90%
let their students do that.

00:44:26.850 --> 00:44:28.822 align:middle line:84%
If teachers force
their students along,

00:44:28.822 --> 00:44:30.780 align:middle line:84%
against the will of the
system, then the system

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:32.640 align:middle line:84%
doesn't do what
it's supposed to do.

00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:33.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I think--

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:35.730 align:middle line:84%
I guess to elaborate
a little bit, why

00:44:35.730 --> 00:44:37.697 align:middle line:84%
on like my experience
was the way it was, was

00:44:37.697 --> 00:44:40.390 align:middle line:84%
like not only were teachers
like in your experience,

00:44:40.390 --> 00:44:43.230 align:middle line:84%
just like doing it randomly,
they weren't incorporating it

00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:45.060 align:middle line:90%
into their curriculum.

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:47.260 align:middle line:84%
They were just like
slapping it on top of it.

00:44:47.260 --> 00:44:49.620 align:middle line:84%
And when students are
already overwhelmed

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:52.300 align:middle line:84%
with after school activities
and things like that,

00:44:52.300 --> 00:44:55.320 align:middle line:84%
it then like-- and it was like
more of a math-based thing

00:44:55.320 --> 00:45:00.870 align:middle line:84%
than English, where you see
some of the benefits early on.

00:45:00.870 --> 00:45:02.975 align:middle line:84%
So it was just kind
of thrown in there,

00:45:02.975 --> 00:45:04.600 align:middle line:84%
but not incorporated
in the curriculum.

00:45:04.600 --> 00:45:05.940 align:middle line:84%
And then it's just
like, essentially,

00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:06.705 align:middle line:90%
became a low priority--

00:45:06.705 --> 00:45:07.538 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good--

00:45:07.538 --> 00:45:09.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And like
for a lot of people.

00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:12.520 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So if a system
like depends on some hybridity,

00:45:12.520 --> 00:45:14.250 align:middle line:84%
if it depends on some things
that are happening in class

00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:15.550 align:middle line:84%
and some things are
happening online--

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:17.830 align:middle line:84%
like if those two pieces
are not fitting together,

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:19.205 align:middle line:84%
it's not going to
accomplish what

00:45:19.205 --> 00:45:20.630 align:middle line:90%
the system wants to accomplish.

00:45:20.630 --> 00:45:21.130 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:45:21.130 --> 00:45:22.588 align:middle line:84%
I'll give you a
funny little piece,

00:45:22.588 --> 00:45:25.180 align:middle line:84%
a little personal piece that is
behind some of the technology.

00:45:25.180 --> 00:45:26.680 align:middle line:84%
I explain some of
this a little bit,

00:45:26.680 --> 00:45:29.730 align:middle line:84%
but my father has more or
less done one cool thing

00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:36.400 align:middle line:84%
in his life, which was to get
a fake person into Princeton.

00:45:36.400 --> 00:45:43.140 align:middle line:84%
He and his frat buddies invented
a person named J David Osnat

00:45:43.140 --> 00:45:47.130 align:middle line:90%
or Joe D Osnat or Joe Does Not.

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:52.237 align:middle line:84%
And they found a friend of
theirs to take the SAT for him.

00:45:52.237 --> 00:45:54.570 align:middle line:84%
They found some friends who
had a frat house in Michigan

00:45:54.570 --> 00:45:56.500 align:middle line:90%
to make up a fake transcript.

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:58.950 align:middle line:84%
They got one of them to
go, and do an interview,

00:45:58.950 --> 00:45:59.980 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:45:59.980 --> 00:46:02.490 align:middle line:84%
And they got this fake
guy into Princeton.

00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:05.490 align:middle line:84%
And they eventually
had to admit it.

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:09.210 align:middle line:84%
And at some point, somebody
was taking one of them

00:46:09.210 --> 00:46:14.010 align:middle line:84%
to the admissions office to
go explain to the admissions

00:46:14.010 --> 00:46:16.570 align:middle line:84%
officer that they had gotten
a fake person into Princeton.

00:46:16.570 --> 00:46:19.028 align:middle line:84%
And this guy started asking
them a whole bunch of questions

00:46:19.028 --> 00:46:21.000 align:middle line:90%
about how they took the SAT.

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:23.640 align:middle line:84%
And it turned out this guy
worked for educational testing

00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:26.590 align:middle line:84%
services, which was also
in Princeton, New Jersey,

00:46:26.590 --> 00:46:29.430 align:middle line:84%
and was trying to figure out
some of the test security things

00:46:29.430 --> 00:46:31.020 align:middle line:90%
that were going on there.

00:46:31.020 --> 00:46:34.620 align:middle line:84%
So as I mentioned last
time, one of the ways

00:46:34.620 --> 00:46:36.990 align:middle line:84%
that you can make
tests more secure

00:46:36.990 --> 00:46:40.600 align:middle line:84%
is that you can make it so that
tests are of equal difficulty,

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:42.660 align:middle line:84%
but they use
different questions.

00:46:42.660 --> 00:46:47.130 align:middle line:84%
So the students
in Boston are not

00:46:47.130 --> 00:46:49.290 align:middle line:84%
taking the exact same test
that students in Hawaii

00:46:49.290 --> 00:46:51.180 align:middle line:84%
will take six hours
later, or students

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:52.680 align:middle line:84%
in other parts of the
world, or things like that.

00:46:52.680 --> 00:46:54.138 align:middle line:84%
In order to be able
to do that, you

00:46:54.138 --> 00:46:58.020 align:middle line:84%
have to be able to create a
mathematical representation

00:46:58.020 --> 00:47:01.620 align:middle line:84%
for any given item of how
difficult that item is.

00:47:01.620 --> 00:47:04.410 align:middle line:84%
What people basically
decided to do

00:47:04.410 --> 00:47:08.850 align:middle line:84%
to solve that is to model
the difficulty of items

00:47:08.850 --> 00:47:10.920 align:middle line:90%
as logistic functions.

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:13.840 align:middle line:84%
So on the x-axis,
you have this thing,

00:47:13.840 --> 00:47:15.845 align:middle line:90%
which is a proficiency score.

00:47:15.845 --> 00:47:18.810 align:middle line:84%
Roughly kind of how hard in
any domain this would be.

00:47:18.810 --> 00:47:21.790 align:middle line:84%
And then the probability
of a correct answer.

00:47:21.790 --> 00:47:27.030 align:middle line:84%
So these are three different
possible distributions of items.

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:31.340 align:middle line:84%
So for instance,
which of these items

00:47:31.340 --> 00:47:33.840 align:middle line:84%
would be the most difficult for
individuals with proficiency

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:34.655 align:middle line:90%
score of 1.

00:47:34.655 --> 00:47:40.110 align:middle line:90%


00:47:40.110 --> 00:47:41.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The blue.

00:47:41.430 --> 00:47:44.670 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So people on
the blue have almost 100%

00:47:44.670 --> 00:47:46.505 align:middle line:84%
probability of getting
this one correct.

00:47:46.505 --> 00:47:47.850 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, I was wondering.

00:47:47.850 --> 00:47:49.600 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So it
will be the green one.

00:47:49.600 --> 00:47:51.080 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
probability answer.

00:47:51.080 --> 00:47:55.640 align:middle line:84%
So for this particular item,
people with proficiency of 1

00:47:55.640 --> 00:47:57.540 align:middle line:84%
are about 50% likely
to get it right.

00:47:57.540 --> 00:48:01.050 align:middle line:84%
These are the people who are
about 90% to get it right,

00:48:01.050 --> 00:48:03.470 align:middle line:84%
almost exactly likely
to get it right.

00:48:03.470 --> 00:48:05.820 align:middle line:84%
So that seems like a useful
thing to know about an item,

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:06.300 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:48:06.300 --> 00:48:07.590 align:middle line:84%
Now you can figure
out, like, oh,

00:48:07.590 --> 00:48:09.500 align:middle line:84%
let's give them the blue
item first, and then

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:13.010 align:middle line:84%
the red item, and then the green
item, or something like that.

00:48:13.010 --> 00:48:15.900 align:middle line:84%
Not only that, but if
you model it this way,

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:19.940 align:middle line:84%
you can also figure out how
discriminating an item is,

00:48:19.940 --> 00:48:23.930 align:middle line:84%
which is how likely is it
that people around a given

00:48:23.930 --> 00:48:28.230 align:middle line:84%
proficiency are likely to get
it wrong or not get it wrong?

00:48:28.230 --> 00:48:33.290 align:middle line:84%
Like, how tightly clustered
are the answers versus be

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:37.040 align:middle line:84%
or how spread out are
the answers going to be?

00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:43.790 align:middle line:84%
So around 1, you would
imagine for this one,

00:48:43.790 --> 00:48:48.810 align:middle line:84%
that a whole bunch of people
with scores slightly less than 1

00:48:48.810 --> 00:48:50.268 align:middle line:90%
would be less likely to write.

00:48:50.268 --> 00:48:51.810 align:middle line:84%
And then more likely
to get it right.

00:48:51.810 --> 00:48:55.730 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, around here, because
the lines are more horizontal

00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:58.010 align:middle line:84%
there, just about everybody
who gets those items are

00:48:58.010 --> 00:48:58.760 align:middle line:90%
going to be right.

00:48:58.760 --> 00:49:01.100 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to give
you that much information

00:49:01.100 --> 00:49:03.883 align:middle line:90%
about how well people do.

00:49:03.883 --> 00:49:05.300 align:middle line:84%
Another way that
you could do this

00:49:05.300 --> 00:49:08.960 align:middle line:84%
is draw a distribution of where
you think a group of learners

00:49:08.960 --> 00:49:09.800 align:middle line:90%
would be.

00:49:09.800 --> 00:49:13.880 align:middle line:84%
So this is like the various
skill levels in the class

00:49:13.880 --> 00:49:15.950 align:middle line:84%
and the distribution of
probabilities of people

00:49:15.950 --> 00:49:17.150 align:middle line:90%
getting it right.

00:49:17.150 --> 00:49:19.310 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
that the item that

00:49:19.310 --> 00:49:22.310 align:middle line:84%
traverse through the
heart of that group

00:49:22.310 --> 00:49:24.980 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the one that's
best at efficiently measuring

00:49:24.980 --> 00:49:26.960 align:middle line:90%
the proficiency of that group.

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:29.635 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to totally
understand all the math or all

00:49:29.635 --> 00:49:30.510 align:middle line:90%
the ideas about this.

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:32.843 align:middle line:84%
Probably, like the intuition
that you need to develop is

00:49:32.843 --> 00:49:36.140 align:middle line:84%
in the 1980s, a bunch of people
came up with a pretty good

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:39.560 align:middle line:84%
statistical toolkit for figuring
out how difficult an item was.

00:49:39.560 --> 00:49:42.020 align:middle line:84%
The first thing that
they did with it was

00:49:42.020 --> 00:49:45.630 align:middle line:84%
make different kinds of tests
for test security purposes.

00:49:45.630 --> 00:49:47.840 align:middle line:84%
But almost the next thing
they did with it was

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:50.600 align:middle line:84%
start developing systems
that use computers

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:53.250 align:middle line:84%
to evaluate people's
performance on an item,

00:49:53.250 --> 00:49:55.665 align:middle line:84%
and then figure out what
is the next good item

00:49:55.665 --> 00:49:56.790 align:middle line:90%
to give them in a sequence.

00:49:56.790 --> 00:49:58.498 align:middle line:84%
And all the things
that you're describing

00:49:58.498 --> 00:49:59.990 align:middle line:84%
IXL, Khan Academy,
all other things

00:49:59.990 --> 00:50:03.360 align:middle line:84%
like that, they may not use
this exact statistical toolkit,

00:50:03.360 --> 00:50:06.685 align:middle line:84%
but they all use something
that's similar to that.

00:50:06.685 --> 00:50:10.460 align:middle line:90%


00:50:10.460 --> 00:50:12.710 align:middle line:84%
I mean, part of the reason
why in the book that I take

00:50:12.710 --> 00:50:15.380 align:middle line:84%
a couple of pages to describe
item response theory to people

00:50:15.380 --> 00:50:18.770 align:middle line:84%
is, again, to try to give
the sense it's not magic.

00:50:18.770 --> 00:50:21.860 align:middle line:84%
It's not like some
impossibly difficult thing

00:50:21.860 --> 00:50:25.730 align:middle line:84%
that computers and scientists
on the very cutting

00:50:25.730 --> 00:50:27.830 align:middle line:84%
edge of mathematics
have figured out.

00:50:27.830 --> 00:50:30.620 align:middle line:84%
This is a pretty
well-established set of tools

00:50:30.620 --> 00:50:33.080 align:middle line:84%
that we've studied
for a long time.

00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:37.080 align:middle line:84%
If people come up with new
AI-powered intelligent tutors,

00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:40.020 align:middle line:84%
we should absolutely be able
to ask the question like, well,

00:50:40.020 --> 00:50:42.395 align:middle line:84%
how are they really different
from the intelligent tutors

00:50:42.395 --> 00:50:44.910 align:middle line:84%
that we've had for decades,
in various formats?

00:50:44.910 --> 00:50:48.830 align:middle line:84%
How different are they from
the box of SRI reading cards

00:50:48.830 --> 00:50:50.850 align:middle line:84%
that I had when I was
in the fourth grade?

00:50:50.850 --> 00:50:52.490 align:middle line:84%
And there may be some
ways that they're

00:50:52.490 --> 00:50:53.510 align:middle line:84%
different, in which
case, we would

00:50:53.510 --> 00:50:54.840 align:middle line:90%
expect much better outcomes.

00:50:54.840 --> 00:50:56.923 align:middle line:84%
There may be some ways
that they're quite similar,

00:50:56.923 --> 00:51:00.470 align:middle line:84%
in which case, we might not
expect that many differences.

00:51:00.470 --> 00:51:04.730 align:middle line:84%
But the heart of the
reason why people

00:51:04.730 --> 00:51:10.490 align:middle line:84%
wanted these kinds of tools
to exist was personalization.

00:51:10.490 --> 00:51:12.680 align:middle line:84%
That a goal that you
can get a lot of people

00:51:12.680 --> 00:51:14.810 align:middle line:84%
around is the idea
that wouldn't it

00:51:14.810 --> 00:51:18.230 align:middle line:84%
be great if every learner
could have an experience that's

00:51:18.230 --> 00:51:23.420 align:middle line:84%
tailored to their own
personal needs, and interests,

00:51:23.420 --> 00:51:25.370 align:middle line:84%
and experiences, and
things like that?

00:51:25.370 --> 00:51:29.270 align:middle line:84%
I noticed, I don't know,
around 2008, 2009, 2010

00:51:29.270 --> 00:51:31.400 align:middle line:84%
that lots of people in
education got interested

00:51:31.400 --> 00:51:32.460 align:middle line:90%
in personalization.

00:51:32.460 --> 00:51:34.295 align:middle line:84%
A thing that was weird
to me is that there

00:51:34.295 --> 00:51:36.420 align:middle line:84%
were people who were
interested in personalization,

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:39.920 align:middle line:84%
who disagreed about
everything else.

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:44.720 align:middle line:84%
So there would be people who
are really into school choice,

00:51:44.720 --> 00:51:49.170 align:middle line:84%
and traditional instruction, and
conservative curriculum values,

00:51:49.170 --> 00:51:51.800 align:middle line:84%
who typically were
more associated

00:51:51.800 --> 00:51:54.230 align:middle line:84%
with people on the political
right, though not always

00:51:54.230 --> 00:51:57.090 align:middle line:84%
in America, who got really
interested in personalization.

00:51:57.090 --> 00:52:00.980 align:middle line:84%
There were also people who were
educational progressives, who

00:52:00.980 --> 00:52:03.980 align:middle line:84%
were really interested in
alternate, project-based school

00:52:03.980 --> 00:52:04.650 align:middle line:90%
kinds of models.

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:07.490 align:middle line:84%
They also were talking
about personalization.

00:52:07.490 --> 00:52:09.260 align:middle line:90%
And I thought to myself, OK.

00:52:09.260 --> 00:52:12.530 align:middle line:84%
If these groups who don't
agree about anything

00:52:12.530 --> 00:52:14.670 align:middle line:84%
are all using the
word personalization,

00:52:14.670 --> 00:52:16.910 align:middle line:84%
they can't possibly
mean the same thing,

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:18.750 align:middle line:84%
because they have
really different values.

00:52:18.750 --> 00:52:21.110 align:middle line:84%
And so they probably don't
agree about this either.

00:52:21.110 --> 00:52:24.470 align:middle line:84%
Oh, this was a slide that my
colleague Dan Meyer put together

00:52:24.470 --> 00:52:27.710 align:middle line:84%
of this is some
technology from the 1970s.

00:52:27.710 --> 00:52:30.950 align:middle line:84%
Right at the dawn of
personal computers, where

00:52:30.950 --> 00:52:34.020 align:middle line:84%
we were trying to give kids
math problems, evaluate them,

00:52:34.020 --> 00:52:35.130 align:middle line:90%
give them harder problems.

00:52:35.130 --> 00:52:37.110 align:middle line:90%
This is a slide from 2012.

00:52:37.110 --> 00:52:38.580 align:middle line:84%
But the thing that
you can see is

00:52:38.580 --> 00:52:41.270 align:middle line:84%
there's 40 years
between these pictures.

00:52:41.270 --> 00:52:48.230 align:middle line:84%
And they are strikingly similar
in their setup, in their outfit.

00:52:48.230 --> 00:52:50.960 align:middle line:84%
Trying to personalize
education with computers

00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:55.525 align:middle line:84%
is one of the oldest enterprises
in educational computing.

00:52:55.525 --> 00:52:57.650 align:middle line:84%
It is one of the main things
that we've been trying

00:52:57.650 --> 00:53:01.400 align:middle line:90%
to do for 40 or 50 years.

00:53:01.400 --> 00:53:04.490 align:middle line:84%
If there is any
single paper that

00:53:04.490 --> 00:53:08.240 align:middle line:84%
got people extremely
excited about this,

00:53:08.240 --> 00:53:10.677 align:middle line:84%
it was called "The
2 Sigma Problem--

00:53:10.677 --> 00:53:13.010 align:middle line:84%
The Search for Methods of
Group Instruction as Effective

00:53:13.010 --> 00:53:15.770 align:middle line:84%
as One-to-One Tutoring,"
where this guy Benjamin--

00:53:15.770 --> 00:53:18.860 align:middle line:84%
any of you ever heard
of Bloom's Taxonomy?

00:53:18.860 --> 00:53:21.710 align:middle line:84%
It's this little
pyramid that has

00:53:21.710 --> 00:53:25.147 align:middle line:84%
different kinds of
learning activities,

00:53:25.147 --> 00:53:26.730 align:middle line:84%
instructional
activities you could do.

00:53:26.730 --> 00:53:28.850 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that Benjamin
Bloom did more than one thing

00:53:28.850 --> 00:53:29.423 align:middle line:90%
in his life.

00:53:29.423 --> 00:53:30.840 align:middle line:84%
He didn't just
make this taxonomy,

00:53:30.840 --> 00:53:32.180 align:middle line:90%
but he other kinds of studies.

00:53:32.180 --> 00:53:33.680 align:middle line:84%
He didn't actually
do these studies.

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:36.560 align:middle line:84%
He was reporting on some
doctoral students of his

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:39.750 align:middle line:90%
that were studying tutoring.

00:53:39.750 --> 00:53:41.850 align:middle line:84%
And in these tutoring
studies that they did,

00:53:41.850 --> 00:53:44.888 align:middle line:84%
you just get astronomically
good results.

00:53:44.888 --> 00:53:46.430 align:middle line:84%
They said that they
could get results

00:53:46.430 --> 00:53:50.520 align:middle line:84%
two standard deviations better
what you'd typically find.

00:53:50.520 --> 00:53:52.430 align:middle line:90%
That's the 2 sigma problem.

00:53:52.430 --> 00:53:54.710 align:middle line:84%
A two standard deviation
improvement in education

00:53:54.710 --> 00:53:57.110 align:middle line:90%
is impossibly good.

00:53:57.110 --> 00:53:59.690 align:middle line:84%
Most of the things
that we do get

00:53:59.690 --> 00:54:01.670 align:middle line:84%
a tenth of a standard
deviation, or a hundredth

00:54:01.670 --> 00:54:03.080 align:middle line:84%
of a standard
deviation improvement,

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:03.750 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:54:03.750 --> 00:54:04.890 align:middle line:84%
We usually get
really small gains,

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:05.940 align:middle line:90%
but tutoring works really well.

00:54:05.940 --> 00:54:07.315 align:middle line:84%
I mean, to some
extent, you could

00:54:07.315 --> 00:54:09.410 align:middle line:84%
be like, I'm pretty
sure medieval lords knew

00:54:09.410 --> 00:54:10.750 align:middle line:90%
that tutoring works pretty well.

00:54:10.750 --> 00:54:11.300 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:11.300 --> 00:54:14.100 align:middle line:84%
The reason why we don't do it
is it's enormously expensive.

00:54:14.100 --> 00:54:16.670 align:middle line:84%
But what if, instead
of medieval lords

00:54:16.670 --> 00:54:19.610 align:middle line:84%
hiring governesses to
tutor their students,

00:54:19.610 --> 00:54:22.160 align:middle line:90%
what if computers could do that?

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:24.658 align:middle line:84%
What if computers, which
are relatively inexpensive,

00:54:24.658 --> 00:54:25.950 align:middle line:90%
could do this kind of tutoring?

00:54:25.950 --> 00:54:27.030 align:middle line:90%
So this has been a dream.

00:54:27.030 --> 00:54:29.430 align:middle line:84%
This was Daphne Coller's
dream, Saul Cotton's dream,

00:54:29.430 --> 00:54:31.640 align:middle line:84%
lots of other people's
dream as they were imagining

00:54:31.640 --> 00:54:33.340 align:middle line:90%
what's possible here.

00:54:33.340 --> 00:54:36.530 align:middle line:90%


00:54:36.530 --> 00:54:39.500 align:middle line:84%
Around 2010, there were
probably two different groups

00:54:39.500 --> 00:54:45.240 align:middle line:84%
of people, who made personalized
learning central to their ideas

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:46.620 align:middle line:90%
and their philosophies.

00:54:46.620 --> 00:54:48.870 align:middle line:84%
One group of people were
really interested in what

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:51.728 align:middle line:84%
they called blended learning--
a formal educational program

00:54:51.728 --> 00:54:53.520 align:middle line:84%
in which a student
learns, at least in part

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:56.490 align:middle line:84%
through online delivery, with
some element of student control

00:54:56.490 --> 00:55:00.090 align:middle line:90%
over time, place, path, or pace.

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:04.800 align:middle line:84%
Some of it may be supervised,
and then some of it at home.

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:08.280 align:middle line:84%
The alternative view
was called connected

00:55:08.280 --> 00:55:10.842 align:middle line:84%
learning-- socially embedded,
interest-driven, oriented

00:55:10.842 --> 00:55:13.050 align:middle line:84%
towards educational, economic,
political opportunity.

00:55:13.050 --> 00:55:14.508 align:middle line:84%
Connected learning
is realized when

00:55:14.508 --> 00:55:16.830 align:middle line:84%
a person is able to
pursue a personal interest

00:55:16.830 --> 00:55:20.790 align:middle line:84%
or passion with the support
of friends and caring adults.

00:55:20.790 --> 00:55:24.395 align:middle line:84%
So what else do these
two ideas sound like?

00:55:24.395 --> 00:55:30.015 align:middle line:90%


00:55:30.015 --> 00:55:31.158 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: CLT.

00:55:31.158 --> 00:55:32.200 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, good.

00:55:32.200 --> 00:55:34.073 align:middle line:84%
So you could have said
cognitive load theory

00:55:34.073 --> 00:55:34.990 align:middle line:90%
and situated learning.

00:55:34.990 --> 00:55:36.220 align:middle line:84%
You could have said
doing Thorndike.

00:55:36.220 --> 00:55:37.637 align:middle line:84%
You could have
said instructionism

00:55:37.637 --> 00:55:38.870 align:middle line:90%
and constructionism.

00:55:38.870 --> 00:55:41.570 align:middle line:84%
This is like the reoccurrence
of these two themes

00:55:41.570 --> 00:55:44.000 align:middle line:84%
over-- like there's
always two ideas.

00:55:44.000 --> 00:55:44.510 align:middle line:90%
And they're.

00:55:44.510 --> 00:55:45.830 align:middle line:84%
And they're always
being reformatted

00:55:45.830 --> 00:55:47.010 align:middle line:84%
and they're always getting
connected to each other.

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:49.370 align:middle line:84%
So this is like the Dewey
and Thorndike version

00:55:49.370 --> 00:55:51.830 align:middle line:84%
of how we're going to
make personalization

00:55:51.830 --> 00:55:54.380 align:middle line:90%
come to life in the world.

00:55:54.380 --> 00:55:57.345 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk more about
connected learning in a week.

00:55:57.345 --> 00:55:58.970 align:middle line:84%
But mostly, what
we'll talk about today

00:55:58.970 --> 00:56:02.160 align:middle line:84%
is what this blended
learning might look like.

00:56:02.160 --> 00:56:04.670 align:middle line:84%
So Khan Academy doesn't
have this anymore.

00:56:04.670 --> 00:56:09.350 align:middle line:84%
But they used to represent
their mathematics content in K

00:56:09.350 --> 00:56:12.920 align:middle line:84%
through 12 as a knowledge
map, as kind of a galaxy.

00:56:12.920 --> 00:56:15.740 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that
this blended learning

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:18.110 align:middle line:84%
version of
personalization requires

00:56:18.110 --> 00:56:19.910 align:middle line:84%
is you have to know
all of the stuff

00:56:19.910 --> 00:56:22.020 align:middle line:84%
that you want people
to learn in advance.

00:56:22.020 --> 00:56:24.800 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to create an
algorithmically optimized

00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:27.840 align:middle line:84%
pathway for people through
a set of learning materials,

00:56:27.840 --> 00:56:30.913 align:middle line:84%
you have to know what those
learning materials are,

00:56:30.913 --> 00:56:33.080 align:middle line:84%
so that you can write the
algorithms that drive them

00:56:33.080 --> 00:56:33.800 align:middle line:90%
through it.

00:56:33.800 --> 00:56:36.680 align:middle line:84%
That's very different from this
connected learning view, which

00:56:36.680 --> 00:56:38.710 align:middle line:84%
says we should figure out what
kids are really interested in

00:56:38.710 --> 00:56:41.230 align:middle line:84%
and help them create personal
pathways through whatever it

00:56:41.230 --> 00:56:43.090 align:middle line:90%
is they want.

00:56:43.090 --> 00:56:45.850 align:middle line:84%
So you can imagine that
the goal of Khan Academy

00:56:45.850 --> 00:56:49.220 align:middle line:84%
is to take some individual kid
and figure out the fastest,

00:56:49.220 --> 00:56:51.760 align:middle line:84%
most efficient way for
them to traverse pathways

00:56:51.760 --> 00:56:53.180 align:middle line:90%
through this kind of thing.

00:56:53.180 --> 00:56:57.100 align:middle line:84%
People were very
compelled by that model.

00:56:57.100 --> 00:56:59.180 align:middle line:90%
Partially, it was interesting.

00:56:59.180 --> 00:57:01.010 align:middle line:90%
They were contrasting it.

00:57:01.010 --> 00:57:03.760 align:middle line:84%
A lot of times
people will critique

00:57:03.760 --> 00:57:06.790 align:middle line:84%
what they call the factory
model of schooling, which

00:57:06.790 --> 00:57:09.040 align:middle line:84%
is that students come
in, in cohorts, they're

00:57:09.040 --> 00:57:10.542 align:middle line:90%
stamped at the same time.

00:57:10.542 --> 00:57:12.250 align:middle line:84%
They're moved through
the same materials,

00:57:12.250 --> 00:57:14.210 align:middle line:84%
despite all their
individual differences.

00:57:14.210 --> 00:57:16.300 align:middle line:84%
It's not really
true, but there are

00:57:16.300 --> 00:57:18.790 align:middle line:84%
a lot of folks who will say
that schools were organized

00:57:18.790 --> 00:57:21.615 align:middle line:84%
around factories, in order
to generate factory laborers

00:57:21.615 --> 00:57:22.490 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:57:22.490 --> 00:57:24.640 align:middle line:84%
And there are certainly
ideas about how

00:57:24.640 --> 00:57:27.610 align:middle line:84%
we use time and other kinds
of things like that go

00:57:27.610 --> 00:57:29.090 align:middle line:90%
across institutions in society.

00:57:29.090 --> 00:57:32.590 align:middle line:84%
This is a representation
from a keynote.

00:57:32.590 --> 00:57:34.210 align:middle line:90%
Oh, Sir Ken.

00:57:34.210 --> 00:57:35.170 align:middle line:90%
I'm forgetting.

00:57:35.170 --> 00:57:39.895 align:middle line:84%
He's the most popular
keynote on TED.

00:57:39.895 --> 00:57:41.770 align:middle line:84%
I still think it's true,
the most popular TED

00:57:41.770 --> 00:57:43.550 align:middle line:90%
Talk that was ever written.

00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:45.550 align:middle line:84%
Somebody will have to
look up Ken and figure out

00:57:45.550 --> 00:57:46.960 align:middle line:90%
who I mean by this.

00:57:46.960 --> 00:57:50.080 align:middle line:84%
But I don't know if this is
actually from that or a follow

00:57:50.080 --> 00:57:51.940 align:middle line:90%
up representation of slide.

00:57:51.940 --> 00:57:58.540 align:middle line:84%
So then the question is does
this blended learning model have

00:57:58.540 --> 00:58:00.830 align:middle line:90%
truly individualized thing?

00:58:00.830 --> 00:58:03.130 align:middle line:84%
Or, are you just kind of
like stamping each child

00:58:03.130 --> 00:58:05.680 align:middle line:84%
with the same stuff
in their own rate?

00:58:05.680 --> 00:58:07.685 align:middle line:84%
They all still are going
to get kind of stamped

00:58:07.685 --> 00:58:09.310 align:middle line:84%
with these materials,
but maybe they'll

00:58:09.310 --> 00:58:13.552 align:middle line:84%
get stamped through the factory
at their own individual path.

00:58:13.552 --> 00:58:14.510 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Ken Robinson.

00:58:14.510 --> 00:58:15.820 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Ken Robinson.

00:58:15.820 --> 00:58:16.600 align:middle line:90%
Robinson.

00:58:16.600 --> 00:58:19.450 align:middle line:90%
Sir Ken Robinson.

00:58:19.450 --> 00:58:24.200 align:middle line:84%
People make all kinds of crazy
claims about these systems.

00:58:24.200 --> 00:58:26.830 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes I like to
share these crazy claims

00:58:26.830 --> 00:58:30.260 align:middle line:84%
10 years later because they
sound silly afterwards.

00:58:30.260 --> 00:58:32.970 align:middle line:84%
So in 2009, Clay
Christensen, who

00:58:32.970 --> 00:58:35.368 align:middle line:84%
was one of the people who
defined blended learning,

00:58:35.368 --> 00:58:37.660 align:middle line:84%
wrote this book called
Disrupting Class in the business

00:58:37.660 --> 00:58:41.030 align:middle line:84%
school, do you study still the
theory of disruptive innovation?

00:58:41.030 --> 00:58:43.210 align:middle line:90%
Is that a thing that--

00:58:43.210 --> 00:58:44.990 align:middle line:84%
it was like crazy
popular a while ago?

00:58:44.990 --> 00:58:46.130 align:middle line:90%
And I think it has faded.

00:58:46.130 --> 00:58:48.130 align:middle line:84%
But they apply this
idea called the theory

00:58:48.130 --> 00:58:49.250 align:middle line:90%
of disruptive innovation.

00:58:49.250 --> 00:58:51.820 align:middle line:84%
And in 2009, they
made the prediction

00:58:51.820 --> 00:58:55.360 align:middle line:84%
that by 2019, 50% of all
grades six through 12

00:58:55.360 --> 00:58:56.360 align:middle line:90%
courses would be online.

00:58:56.360 --> 00:58:58.870 align:middle line:84%
That they would cost a
third as much to deliver.

00:58:58.870 --> 00:59:01.390 align:middle line:84%
That your school system
would cost like about a third

00:59:01.390 --> 00:59:03.500 align:middle line:84%
as much to operate
as it does right now.

00:59:03.500 --> 00:59:05.447 align:middle line:84%
And they would have
better learning outcomes.

00:59:05.447 --> 00:59:07.030 align:middle line:84%
How many of you were
in a school where

00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:10.120 align:middle line:84%
50% of all of your classes
in secondary school

00:59:10.120 --> 00:59:12.890 align:middle line:84%
were online or
predominantly online?

00:59:12.890 --> 00:59:13.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, zero.

00:59:13.700 --> 00:59:15.580 align:middle line:84%
Pretty much didn't
work out that way.

00:59:15.580 --> 00:59:19.330 align:middle line:84%
Sebastian Thrun, the quote
that Dana saw, was up there.

00:59:19.330 --> 00:59:23.140 align:middle line:84%
Sal Khan had an extremely
popular TED Talk called

00:59:23.140 --> 00:59:25.460 align:middle line:84%
"Let's Use Video to
Reinvent Education,"

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:29.290 align:middle line:84%
where he described this vision
where kids would sit down

00:59:29.290 --> 00:59:30.800 align:middle line:84%
in front of
individual terminals.

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:33.560 align:middle line:84%
They would learn at an
algorithmically optimized rate

00:59:33.560 --> 00:59:34.890 align:middle line:90%
through mathematical content.

00:59:34.890 --> 00:59:36.470 align:middle line:84%
They still might
do things together.

00:59:36.470 --> 00:59:38.060 align:middle line:84%
A teacher might come
and collect students

00:59:38.060 --> 00:59:40.470 align:middle line:84%
who are stuck in the same thing
and remediate them together.

00:59:40.470 --> 00:59:43.053 align:middle line:84%
Or, take advanced students and
do projects with them or things

00:59:43.053 --> 00:59:43.590 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:59:43.590 --> 00:59:45.530 align:middle line:84%
But the heart of their
mathematical learning

00:59:45.530 --> 00:59:47.330 align:middle line:90%
would be individual.

00:59:47.330 --> 00:59:49.200 align:middle line:84%
There's one guy
who was actually,

00:59:49.200 --> 00:59:51.600 align:middle line:84%
I think, probably the
most wrong of them,

00:59:51.600 --> 00:59:54.270 align:middle line:90%
who won the 2013 Ted Prize.

00:59:54.270 --> 00:59:56.780 align:middle line:90%
He got $1 million for this idea.

00:59:56.780 --> 01:00:00.090 align:middle line:84%
That you don't even need
schools or educational systems.

01:00:00.090 --> 01:00:01.820 align:middle line:84%
If you just give
kids laptops, they

01:00:01.820 --> 01:00:04.280 align:middle line:84%
can teach anything
by themselves.

01:00:04.280 --> 01:00:07.220 align:middle line:84%
And he had this whole program
called The Hole in the Wall

01:00:07.220 --> 01:00:09.740 align:middle line:84%
Program, where they put
computers in public spaces

01:00:09.740 --> 01:00:11.810 align:middle line:90%
in Southeast Asia.

01:00:11.810 --> 01:00:12.550 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

01:00:12.550 --> 01:00:15.050 align:middle line:84%
There are millions of parents
who lived through the pandemic

01:00:15.050 --> 01:00:17.772 align:middle line:84%
that can confirm that kids with
laptops cannot teach anything

01:00:17.772 --> 01:00:18.355 align:middle line:90%
to themselves.

01:00:18.355 --> 01:00:20.030 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:00:20.030 --> 01:00:22.070 align:middle line:84%
Sal Khan is interesting
to me because there

01:00:22.070 --> 01:00:26.630 align:middle line:84%
are millions of people who
have watched his 2011 TED

01:00:26.630 --> 01:00:28.020 align:middle line:90%
Talk about this model.

01:00:28.020 --> 01:00:29.750 align:middle line:84%
There are many,
many fewer people

01:00:29.750 --> 01:00:32.840 align:middle line:84%
who read an interview that
he did in 2019 with District

01:00:32.840 --> 01:00:34.370 align:middle line:90%
Administration Magazine.

01:00:34.370 --> 01:00:37.160 align:middle line:84%
So District Administration is
like a little trade magazine

01:00:37.160 --> 01:00:38.810 align:middle line:90%
for superintendents.

01:00:38.810 --> 01:00:42.690 align:middle line:84%
In between starting
Khan Academy in 2019,

01:00:42.690 --> 01:00:45.800 align:middle line:84%
he built his own school
called the Khan Lab School.

01:00:45.800 --> 01:00:47.262 align:middle line:90%
It's a private school.

01:00:47.262 --> 01:00:48.720 align:middle line:84%
It's in the Bay
Area of California.

01:00:48.720 --> 01:00:51.303 align:middle line:84%
It costs $20,000 or $30,000 or
something like that to go there

01:00:51.303 --> 01:00:51.930 align:middle line:90%
for a year.

01:00:51.930 --> 01:00:53.347 align:middle line:84%
But it's basically
his laboratory.

01:00:53.347 --> 01:00:55.443 align:middle line:84%
He can implement
any model he wanted.

01:00:55.443 --> 01:00:56.610 align:middle line:90%
He can pick his own faculty.

01:00:56.610 --> 01:00:57.902 align:middle line:90%
He can pick his own curriculum.

01:00:57.902 --> 01:01:00.065 align:middle line:84%
He can recruit whatever
students he wants.

01:01:00.065 --> 01:01:01.440 align:middle line:84%
And he does this
for a few years.

01:01:01.440 --> 01:01:03.120 align:middle line:84%
And he says, now
that I run a school,

01:01:03.120 --> 01:01:04.250 align:middle line:84%
I see that some of
this stuff is not

01:01:04.250 --> 01:01:06.720 align:middle line:84%
as easy to accomplish compared
to how it sounds theoretically.

01:01:06.720 --> 01:01:08.940 align:middle line:84%
It's taken me and the Khan
Academy longer to realize this,

01:01:08.940 --> 01:01:11.618 align:middle line:84%
but not everyone can easily move
to a mastery-based, self-paced

01:01:11.618 --> 01:01:12.660 align:middle line:90%
learning model overnight.

01:01:12.660 --> 01:01:13.560 align:middle line:90%
That's hard to do.

01:01:13.560 --> 01:01:15.780 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of efficacy
studies for that type of model,

01:01:15.780 --> 01:01:17.405 align:middle line:84%
and they're robust,
but change is hard.

01:01:17.405 --> 01:01:19.850 align:middle line:84%
More recently, we're seeing
that students put 30 minutes

01:01:19.850 --> 01:01:22.107 align:middle line:84%
to an hour per week, or
one class period per week

01:01:22.107 --> 01:01:23.940 align:middle line:84%
towards software-based,
self-paced learning.

01:01:23.940 --> 01:01:26.270 align:middle line:84%
Schools will see a 20% to 30%
greater-than-expected gain

01:01:26.270 --> 01:01:27.060 align:middle line:90%
on state assessments.

01:01:27.060 --> 01:01:28.977 align:middle line:84%
That's exciting because
that's a dosage that's

01:01:28.977 --> 01:01:30.662 align:middle line:84%
very doable in
mainstream classrooms.

01:01:30.662 --> 01:01:33.370 align:middle line:84%
We tell schools to give students
30 to 60 minutes of Khan Academy

01:01:33.370 --> 01:01:35.650 align:middle line:84%
per week, with teachers doing
traditional curriculum four

01:01:35.650 --> 01:01:36.170 align:middle line:90%
days a week.

01:01:36.170 --> 01:01:38.045 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to get
dramatic improvement.

01:01:38.045 --> 01:01:39.940 align:middle line:84%
You'll get the best
of both worlds.

01:01:39.940 --> 01:01:45.280 align:middle line:84%
So this is a pretty big step
away from let's reinvent video

01:01:45.280 --> 01:01:46.570 align:middle line:90%
with education.

01:01:46.570 --> 01:01:48.850 align:middle line:84%
It's saying, like instead
of reinventing education

01:01:48.850 --> 01:01:51.910 align:middle line:84%
with video, let's do practice
problems one day a week.

01:01:51.910 --> 01:01:55.780 align:middle line:84%
And again, you can think about,
is this disrupting education

01:01:55.780 --> 01:01:57.560 align:middle line:84%
or is education
domesticating the system?

01:01:57.560 --> 01:01:59.102 align:middle line:84%
But there's something
which struck me

01:01:59.102 --> 01:02:00.670 align:middle line:84%
about this argument,
which is like, I

01:02:00.670 --> 01:02:02.560 align:middle line:90%
think I've heard this before.

01:02:02.560 --> 01:02:06.580 align:middle line:84%
And one of the earliest places
you can find it is in 1994.

01:02:06.580 --> 01:02:10.030 align:middle line:84%
So there are a group of
folks at Carnegie Mellon, who

01:02:10.030 --> 01:02:13.035 align:middle line:84%
took a bunch of computers
that had digital--

01:02:13.035 --> 01:02:14.660 align:middle line:84%
they wouldn't have
been online network.

01:02:14.660 --> 01:02:16.618 align:middle line:84%
They would have been
digital practice problems.

01:02:16.618 --> 01:02:18.700 align:middle line:84%
Brought them into the
Pittsburgh Public Schools.

01:02:18.700 --> 01:02:21.320 align:middle line:84%
And said to the teachers, hey,
teach regularly three days

01:02:21.320 --> 01:02:21.820 align:middle line:90%
a week.

01:02:21.820 --> 01:02:23.173 align:middle line:90%
And do this two days a week.

01:02:23.173 --> 01:02:24.590 align:middle line:84%
And apparently,
for the most part,

01:02:24.590 --> 01:02:27.160 align:middle line:84%
the teachers were like, eh,
we'll do it one day a week.

01:02:27.160 --> 01:02:32.830 align:middle line:84%
And they got the experimental
classes outperformed students

01:02:32.830 --> 01:02:36.100 align:middle line:84%
in comparison classes by 15%
on standardized tests and 100%

01:02:36.100 --> 01:02:37.760 align:middle line:84%
on tests targeting
these objectives.

01:02:37.760 --> 01:02:39.970 align:middle line:84%
So roughly the same
order of magnitude

01:02:39.970 --> 01:02:41.980 align:middle line:84%
kind of gains that
Khan Academy thought.

01:02:41.980 --> 01:02:44.200 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, one of the ways
that you could frame that

01:02:44.200 --> 01:02:47.590 align:middle line:84%
is that with 10 years
and $150 million

01:02:47.590 --> 01:02:49.630 align:middle line:84%
of philanthropic
investment, Sal Khan

01:02:49.630 --> 01:02:51.588 align:middle line:84%
learned what you could
have learned with a trip

01:02:51.588 --> 01:02:52.630 align:middle line:90%
to the library--

01:02:52.630 --> 01:02:55.690 align:middle line:84%
which is that they're already
existing models for how

01:02:55.690 --> 01:02:58.270 align:middle line:84%
you can incorporate intelligent
tutors and practice problems

01:02:58.270 --> 01:02:59.228 align:middle line:90%
into what you're doing.

01:02:59.228 --> 01:03:00.418 align:middle line:90%
And they work pretty well.

01:03:00.418 --> 01:03:01.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm curious
to know-- so we

01:03:01.960 --> 01:03:05.980 align:middle line:84%
discussed that this
individualized tutoring has

01:03:05.980 --> 01:03:07.863 align:middle line:90%
many benefits.

01:03:07.863 --> 01:03:09.280 align:middle line:84%
But I'm curious
to know if there's

01:03:09.280 --> 01:03:12.910 align:middle line:84%
any studies that show to
which particular subjects

01:03:12.910 --> 01:03:14.525 align:middle line:84%
they might be more
effective or not?

01:03:14.525 --> 01:03:15.650 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: That's great.

01:03:15.650 --> 01:03:15.790 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:03:15.790 --> 01:03:16.295 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:03:16.295 --> 01:03:16.795 align:middle line:90%
Many.

01:03:16.795 --> 01:03:19.660 align:middle line:90%


01:03:19.660 --> 01:03:21.765 align:middle line:90%
It really mostly works in math.

01:03:21.765 --> 01:03:24.670 align:middle line:90%


01:03:24.670 --> 01:03:29.560 align:middle line:84%
The current evidence based
for reading is very weak.

01:03:29.560 --> 01:03:31.370 align:middle line:84%
And we'll show more
examples of that

01:03:31.370 --> 01:03:35.770 align:middle line:84%
and read some more papers
about that for next class.

01:03:35.770 --> 01:03:36.520 align:middle line:90%
So here's a trick.

01:03:36.520 --> 01:03:38.740 align:middle line:84%
When we try to
evaluate these tools,

01:03:38.740 --> 01:03:41.110 align:middle line:84%
you don't want to look at
any individual study, A,

01:03:41.110 --> 01:03:44.392 align:middle line:84%
because any individual
study can be wrong--

01:03:44.392 --> 01:03:45.850 align:middle line:84%
well, maybe that's
the main reason.

01:03:45.850 --> 01:03:47.267 align:middle line:84%
Any individual
study can be wrong.

01:03:47.267 --> 01:03:49.910 align:middle line:84%
So what you do is you do
what's called a meta analysis.

01:03:49.910 --> 01:03:51.290 align:middle line:90%
You look at groups of studies.

01:03:51.290 --> 01:03:53.020 align:middle line:84%
You say, what are
all the studies

01:03:53.020 --> 01:03:55.250 align:middle line:84%
that have been done with
intelligent tutors in math?

01:03:55.250 --> 01:03:56.170 align:middle line:84%
What are all the
studies that have

01:03:56.170 --> 01:03:58.100 align:middle line:84%
been done with intelligent
tutors in reading?

01:03:58.100 --> 01:04:00.700 align:middle line:84%
And generally speaking,
the only places

01:04:00.700 --> 01:04:05.020 align:middle line:84%
that we find consistently more
positive things, which are not

01:04:05.020 --> 01:04:07.030 align:middle line:90%
hugely positive, is in math.

01:04:07.030 --> 01:04:09.220 align:middle line:84%
What complicates
that a little bit

01:04:09.220 --> 01:04:14.020 align:middle line:84%
is that some of the
strongest studies in evidence

01:04:14.020 --> 01:04:16.360 align:middle line:84%
for mathematics have
been more recently.

01:04:16.360 --> 01:04:18.650 align:middle line:84%
Now, one possibility
is that that's a fluke.

01:04:18.650 --> 01:04:21.050 align:middle line:84%
Another possibility is that
we're figuring stuff out.

01:04:21.050 --> 01:04:21.970 align:middle line:84%
So you have to
decide to yourself,

01:04:21.970 --> 01:04:24.512 align:middle line:84%
like do you want to average over
the whole length of studies?

01:04:24.512 --> 01:04:26.400 align:middle line:84%
Or, do you want to
reinforce your ideas

01:04:26.400 --> 01:04:27.580 align:middle line:90%
based on more recent things.

01:04:27.580 --> 01:04:28.680 align:middle line:84%
I'll make an
argument next class.

01:04:28.680 --> 01:04:30.940 align:middle line:84%
That actually some of the
recent studies in math

01:04:30.940 --> 01:04:32.550 align:middle line:90%
seem to be pretty robust.

01:04:32.550 --> 01:04:35.460 align:middle line:84%
People try this stuff in
early language acquisition.

01:04:35.460 --> 01:04:37.048 align:middle line:84%
Duolingo, I think,
has some evidence

01:04:37.048 --> 01:04:39.090 align:middle line:84%
that they can teach you
about as much in Duolingo

01:04:39.090 --> 01:04:41.310 align:middle line:84%
as you learn in a first
year college class.

01:04:41.310 --> 01:04:42.420 align:middle line:84%
Although, you should
probably note that

01:04:42.420 --> 01:04:44.490 align:middle line:84%
you really don't learn
that much in a first year

01:04:44.490 --> 01:04:45.965 align:middle line:90%
college class in the language.

01:04:45.965 --> 01:04:46.920 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:49.180 align:middle line:84%
Computer science uses
tons of things like this.

01:04:49.180 --> 01:04:50.880 align:middle line:84%
Probably many of you who've
done computer programming

01:04:50.880 --> 01:04:52.922 align:middle line:84%
with Cat Soup and things
like that, they probably

01:04:52.922 --> 01:04:57.280 align:middle line:84%
have hinting and other sorts of
things that are baked into that.

01:04:57.280 --> 01:05:01.050 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't seem to be very involved
in later language acquisition.

01:05:01.050 --> 01:05:04.500 align:middle line:84%
People don't use it that much
in science or in social studies.

01:05:04.500 --> 01:05:06.113 align:middle line:84%
A little bit in
chemistry, physics.

01:05:06.113 --> 01:05:08.530 align:middle line:84%
Some places have some practice
problems, things like that,

01:05:08.530 --> 01:05:11.370 align:middle line:84%
but not about the conceptual
understanding more

01:05:11.370 --> 01:05:15.570 align:middle line:84%
about the procedural
fluency kinds of things.

01:05:15.570 --> 01:05:22.020 align:middle line:84%
And so what they require, in
addition to these item response

01:05:22.020 --> 01:05:24.395 align:middle line:84%
theory models, which figure
out how difficult an item is,

01:05:24.395 --> 01:05:26.270 align:middle line:84%
is it has to be able
to be auto-graded.

01:05:26.270 --> 01:05:29.352 align:middle line:84%
And because it has to
be able to instantly--

01:05:29.352 --> 01:05:31.060 align:middle line:84%
it has to be able to
instantly assign you

01:05:31.060 --> 01:05:32.530 align:middle line:90%
what the next item is.

01:05:32.530 --> 01:05:35.860 align:middle line:84%
And so the only way we can
do that is we choose items

01:05:35.860 --> 01:05:38.553 align:middle line:84%
where we can be pretty confident
that a machine can ascertain

01:05:38.553 --> 01:05:40.220 align:middle line:84%
whether or not you've
done it correctly.

01:05:40.220 --> 01:05:42.303 align:middle line:84%
You can do that in any
field with multiple choice.

01:05:42.303 --> 01:05:44.480 align:middle line:84%
But multiple choice is--
it's good for some things.

01:05:44.480 --> 01:05:45.670 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of boring.

01:05:45.670 --> 01:05:47.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, I
was going to ask.

01:05:47.230 --> 01:05:49.670 align:middle line:84%
When you said that it's
good with working with math,

01:05:49.670 --> 01:05:53.600 align:middle line:84%
I guess, you answered it
later in your speech just now.

01:05:53.600 --> 01:05:57.070 align:middle line:84%
But what do you mean
by it's good with math

01:05:57.070 --> 01:05:59.290 align:middle line:84%
because there's a difference,
a really strong one,

01:05:59.290 --> 01:06:02.330 align:middle line:84%
between procedural fluency and
like conceptual understanding.

01:06:02.330 --> 01:06:05.870 align:middle line:84%
And when I was thinking
about IXL writing this thing,

01:06:05.870 --> 01:06:06.820 align:middle line:90%
I actually loved IXL.

01:06:06.820 --> 01:06:07.570 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yep.

01:06:07.570 --> 01:06:11.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And the reason was
because my math teachers were

01:06:11.680 --> 01:06:14.130 align:middle line:84%
trying really hard,
but it was boring.

01:06:14.130 --> 01:06:14.630 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:14.630 --> 01:06:17.110 align:middle line:84%
And I felt it was like
something I could use,

01:06:17.110 --> 01:06:19.580 align:middle line:84%
where you don't have
to do the same grade.

01:06:19.580 --> 01:06:21.223 align:middle line:90%
You can go past your grade.

01:06:21.223 --> 01:06:21.530 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, yeah.

01:06:21.530 --> 01:06:23.280 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And then you learn
a bunch of procedural stuff

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:25.197 align:middle line:84%
and you feel like you're
so smart or whatever.

01:06:25.197 --> 01:06:27.675 align:middle line:84%
But that's not true, because
procedural fluency is not--

01:06:27.675 --> 01:06:28.290 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It's
not mathematics.

01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:29.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, it's
definitely-- yeah,

01:06:29.860 --> 01:06:30.730 align:middle line:90%
it's not really math.

01:06:30.730 --> 01:06:32.370 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I mean, a riff
that I'll give you a little bit

01:06:32.370 --> 01:06:34.800 align:middle line:84%
later in the semester-- so
in the Common Core State

01:06:34.800 --> 01:06:38.070 align:middle line:84%
Standards for Mathematics
describe mathematical modeling

01:06:38.070 --> 01:06:39.130 align:middle line:90%
as five steps.

01:06:39.130 --> 01:06:41.010 align:middle line:84%
There are five things
mathematicians do.

01:06:41.010 --> 01:06:42.970 align:middle line:84%
They find a problem
in the real world.

01:06:42.970 --> 01:06:45.730 align:middle line:84%
They arrange that problem
in some kind of structure,

01:06:45.730 --> 01:06:48.490 align:middle line:84%
like an equation, or a
table, or a schematic.

01:06:48.490 --> 01:06:51.340 align:middle line:84%
They resolve the computation
in that structure.

01:06:51.340 --> 01:06:54.390 align:middle line:84%
They make a claim about
how the answer relates

01:06:54.390 --> 01:06:55.210 align:middle line:90%
to the real world.

01:06:55.210 --> 01:06:58.920 align:middle line:84%
So it's 47 board feet, or
47 kittens, or 47 acres.

01:06:58.920 --> 01:07:01.440 align:middle line:84%
And then they explain
their reasoning.

01:07:01.440 --> 01:07:05.280 align:middle line:84%
Our auto-graders can only
assess one of those five things.

01:07:05.280 --> 01:07:06.960 align:middle line:84%
Our auto-graders
basically can only

01:07:06.960 --> 01:07:10.430 align:middle line:84%
assess whether or not you like
address the computational part

01:07:10.430 --> 01:07:10.930 align:middle line:90%
of things.

01:07:10.930 --> 01:07:12.805 align:middle line:84%
They basically don't
work for the other four.

01:07:12.805 --> 01:07:17.370 align:middle line:84%
If you use mathematics
professionally,

01:07:17.370 --> 01:07:19.590 align:middle line:84%
the only value you
have, as a human being,

01:07:19.590 --> 01:07:22.047 align:middle line:84%
are in the four things the
graders can't evaluate.

01:07:22.047 --> 01:07:23.630 align:middle line:84%
If you use mathematics
professionally,

01:07:23.630 --> 01:07:25.210 align:middle line:84%
you probably have
machines compute

01:07:25.210 --> 01:07:27.250 align:middle line:84%
all the answers of the
things that you're doing.

01:07:27.250 --> 01:07:28.808 align:middle line:84%
The value that you,
as a human, bring

01:07:28.808 --> 01:07:30.850 align:middle line:84%
is in figuring out what
problems are interesting,

01:07:30.850 --> 01:07:33.010 align:middle line:84%
and setting them up, and
explaining them to other people,

01:07:33.010 --> 01:07:34.880 align:middle line:84%
and putting the answer
into some kind of context.

01:07:34.880 --> 01:07:36.338 align:middle line:84%
So I think you're
absolutely right.

01:07:36.338 --> 01:07:39.130 align:middle line:84%
Now, what do we mean when
we say it improves math?

01:07:39.130 --> 01:07:42.460 align:middle line:84%
Almost always what we mean is
that in a randomized controlled

01:07:42.460 --> 01:07:47.360 align:middle line:84%
trial, people in the
control condition

01:07:47.360 --> 01:07:51.200 align:middle line:84%
get some average score, usually
on an annual state test.

01:07:51.200 --> 01:07:52.960 align:middle line:84%
And people in the
experimental condition

01:07:52.960 --> 01:07:56.510 align:middle line:84%
get a higher average score,
again, on the same state test.

01:07:56.510 --> 01:07:58.810 align:middle line:84%
That's what we usually
mean by math that works--

01:07:58.810 --> 01:08:01.150 align:middle line:84%
by having a thing work in
math, or any other subject,

01:08:01.150 --> 01:08:01.700 align:middle line:90%
or things like that.

01:08:01.700 --> 01:08:02.750 align:middle line:90%
We might not use a state test.

01:08:02.750 --> 01:08:04.750 align:middle line:84%
We might use some other
particular evaluation or things

01:08:04.750 --> 01:08:05.180 align:middle line:90%
like that.

01:08:05.180 --> 01:08:07.700 align:middle line:84%
But that's almost always the
way these things are evaluated.

01:08:07.700 --> 01:08:08.840 align:middle line:90%
And if those tests--

01:08:08.840 --> 01:08:10.557 align:middle line:84%
those tests are
called math tests--

01:08:10.557 --> 01:08:12.140 align:middle line:84%
but they don't test
you on everything,

01:08:12.140 --> 01:08:13.470 align:middle line:90%
that's important in math.

01:08:13.470 --> 01:08:15.470 align:middle line:84%
And we'll get into that
more as we go along too.

01:08:15.470 --> 01:08:17.390 align:middle line:84%
So you've identified
a crucial issue.

01:08:17.390 --> 01:08:19.098 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to give
you a few other things

01:08:19.098 --> 01:08:21.390 align:middle line:90%
to send you on your way with.

01:08:21.390 --> 01:08:24.240 align:middle line:84%
There is a wonderful book by
Morgan Ames called The Charisma

01:08:24.240 --> 01:08:28.327 align:middle line:84%
Machine, which studies the
One Laptop Per Child Program.

01:08:28.327 --> 01:08:29.910 align:middle line:84%
It studies a particular
implementation

01:08:29.910 --> 01:08:31.785 align:middle line:84%
in Paraguay, which was
kind of a cool country

01:08:31.785 --> 01:08:35.873 align:middle line:84%
to study because you basically
can know everything that's

01:08:35.873 --> 01:08:36.790 align:middle line:90%
happening in Paraguay.

01:08:36.790 --> 01:08:38.310 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of a small place.

01:08:38.310 --> 01:08:40.740 align:middle line:90%
And Morgan was embedded there.

01:08:40.740 --> 01:08:44.399 align:middle line:84%
She argues that there are two
kinds of stances that people

01:08:44.399 --> 01:08:45.670 align:middle line:90%
can take towards technology.

01:08:45.670 --> 01:08:47.520 align:middle line:84%
One is the charismatic
stance, the idea

01:08:47.520 --> 01:08:50.250 align:middle line:84%
that these technologies are
going to be new, and different,

01:08:50.250 --> 01:08:51.122 align:middle line:90%
and transformative.

01:08:51.122 --> 01:08:53.580 align:middle line:84%
And that the future will be
brand new and different because

01:08:53.580 --> 01:08:54.279 align:middle line:90%
of these things.

01:08:54.279 --> 01:08:57.210 align:middle line:84%
And that's what Sal Khan
sounds like in his TED Talk.

01:08:57.210 --> 01:08:59.800 align:middle line:84%
An alternative stance
is the tinkering stance,

01:08:59.800 --> 01:09:02.040 align:middle line:84%
which is what Sal Khan
stands like sounds

01:09:02.040 --> 01:09:06.420 align:middle line:84%
like in his district
administrative interview.

01:09:06.420 --> 01:09:09.060 align:middle line:84%
That existing systems
domesticate new technologies

01:09:09.060 --> 01:09:11.880 align:middle line:84%
that a pretty good guide
for the future of schooling

01:09:11.880 --> 01:09:12.520 align:middle line:90%
and education.

01:09:12.520 --> 01:09:13.979 align:middle line:90%
Technology is the past.

01:09:13.979 --> 01:09:16.390 align:middle line:84%
And it's not that we
can't make things better.

01:09:16.390 --> 01:09:18.090 align:middle line:84%
But that making
things better is hard,

01:09:18.090 --> 01:09:20.842 align:middle line:84%
and it takes a lot of time, and
it involves building capacity

01:09:20.842 --> 01:09:21.550 align:middle line:90%
of the community.

01:09:21.550 --> 01:09:24.840 align:middle line:84%
You could think of
failure to disrupt

01:09:24.840 --> 01:09:28.142 align:middle line:84%
is basically a celebration of
the tinkering stance, which

01:09:28.142 --> 01:09:29.350 align:middle line:90%
you don't have to agree with.

01:09:29.350 --> 01:09:31.830 align:middle line:84%
But one of the hearts
of the argument

01:09:31.830 --> 01:09:35.250 align:middle line:84%
is that charismatics don't
really help education systems.

01:09:35.250 --> 01:09:38.649 align:middle line:84%
Making huge promises that we
can't deliver on is not helpful.

01:09:38.649 --> 01:09:41.734 align:middle line:84%
It diverts resources away from
where they could be most useful.

01:09:41.734 --> 01:09:43.109 align:middle line:84%
A much more useful
thing to do is

01:09:43.109 --> 01:09:45.779 align:middle line:84%
to be real realistic with
school systems about what's

01:09:45.779 --> 01:09:49.710 align:middle line:84%
possible to accept the fact that
usually the things we improve

01:09:49.710 --> 01:09:52.952 align:middle line:84%
are only in some
subjects, but not others.

01:09:52.952 --> 01:09:54.910 align:middle line:84%
And not even in the
entirety of those subjects.

01:09:54.910 --> 01:09:56.260 align:middle line:84%
But only in part
of those subjects.

01:09:56.260 --> 01:09:57.660 align:middle line:84%
And only for certain
kinds of students

01:09:57.660 --> 01:09:58.868 align:middle line:90%
in certain kinds of contexts.

01:09:58.868 --> 01:10:01.020 align:middle line:84%
And it's still worth
pursuing those things

01:10:01.020 --> 01:10:03.990 align:middle line:84%
because the way you make
education systems better is you

01:10:03.990 --> 01:10:07.290 align:middle line:84%
just relentlessly try to
improve little bits of them

01:10:07.290 --> 01:10:09.460 align:middle line:84%
by a little amount over,
and over, and over again.

01:10:09.460 --> 01:10:11.400 align:middle line:84%
If you do that long
enough, they get better.

01:10:11.400 --> 01:10:16.380 align:middle line:84%
But if you want the underlying
argument behind the whole book

01:10:16.380 --> 01:10:19.320 align:middle line:84%
is like stop trying to
disrupt things and instead,

01:10:19.320 --> 01:10:22.380 align:middle line:84%
accept the limits of
what can be accomplished

01:10:22.380 --> 01:10:23.860 align:middle line:90%
with education technology.

01:10:23.860 --> 01:10:26.820 align:middle line:84%
But also, recognize that even
if the improvements we can make

01:10:26.820 --> 01:10:28.927 align:middle line:84%
are small, small
improvements compounded

01:10:28.927 --> 01:10:31.260 align:middle line:84%
over lots of students, over
a long period of time really

01:10:31.260 --> 01:10:31.760 align:middle line:90%
matters.

01:10:31.760 --> 01:10:35.190 align:middle line:90%


01:10:35.190 --> 01:10:37.630 align:middle line:84%
I want to say, briefly, and
maybe I won't finish this,

01:10:37.630 --> 01:10:42.340 align:middle line:84%
but personalization
was a pedagogical idea.

01:10:42.340 --> 01:10:45.510 align:middle line:84%
But it's also connected to
broader political economies.

01:10:45.510 --> 01:10:47.010 align:middle line:84%
I think I first
brought this up when

01:10:47.010 --> 01:10:49.800 align:middle line:84%
we talked about instructionism
and constructionism too.

01:10:49.800 --> 01:10:53.050 align:middle line:84%
That these pedagogies can
be aligned with values.

01:10:53.050 --> 01:10:55.240 align:middle line:84%
The instructionists
say learning is hard.

01:10:55.240 --> 01:10:58.350 align:middle line:84%
The constructionists say
learning is natural and easy.

01:10:58.350 --> 01:11:01.270 align:middle line:84%
That therefore, schools
should be disciplined,

01:11:01.270 --> 01:11:02.880 align:middle line:90%
rigorous, controlled.

01:11:02.880 --> 01:11:05.668 align:middle line:84%
Schools should be free,
democracy-inducing.

01:11:05.668 --> 01:11:07.960 align:middle line:84%
There are values that get
associated with these things.

01:11:07.960 --> 01:11:10.290 align:middle line:84%
And there were values
that got associated

01:11:10.290 --> 01:11:13.410 align:middle line:84%
with personalization, in
particular, the emphasis

01:11:13.410 --> 01:11:17.850 align:middle line:84%
on the individual as the
main unit of analysis

01:11:17.850 --> 01:11:20.040 align:middle line:84%
when thinking about
how to improve schools.

01:11:20.040 --> 01:11:22.000 align:middle line:84%
To me, it's not at all
clear that that's right.

01:11:22.000 --> 01:11:24.190 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that people
thought about a lot--

01:11:24.190 --> 01:11:26.732 align:middle line:84%
and this related to MOOCs, and
intelligent tutors, and things

01:11:26.732 --> 01:11:28.980 align:middle line:84%
like that-- is folks have
said like, it's crazy

01:11:28.980 --> 01:11:31.270 align:middle line:84%
how many different things
we ask teachers to do.

01:11:31.270 --> 01:11:32.970 align:middle line:84%
They need to design
assessments, design

01:11:32.970 --> 01:11:35.760 align:middle line:84%
lessons, grade assessments,
give instruction, coach people,

01:11:35.760 --> 01:11:37.860 align:middle line:84%
provide what the British
call pastoral care, which

01:11:37.860 --> 01:11:39.750 align:middle line:84%
is like taking care of people,
and making them feel good,

01:11:39.750 --> 01:11:41.340 align:middle line:84%
and helping them when they
cry, and put on Band-Aids,

01:11:41.340 --> 01:11:42.215 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

01:11:42.215 --> 01:11:43.920 align:middle line:84%
Why on Earth would
we expect an adult

01:11:43.920 --> 01:11:45.660 align:middle line:90%
to be good at all those things?

01:11:45.660 --> 01:11:48.790 align:middle line:84%
What if some people are just
really good instructors?

01:11:48.790 --> 01:11:50.790 align:middle line:84%
What if we just have
these rock star teachers,

01:11:50.790 --> 01:11:52.470 align:middle line:84%
like Sal Khan and
Sebastian Thrun, who

01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:54.370 align:middle line:84%
are just better at doing
direct instruction?

01:11:54.370 --> 01:11:56.460 align:middle line:84%
Why are we asking every
teacher in the country

01:11:56.460 --> 01:11:59.040 align:middle line:84%
to be able to explain
math, when Sal Khan is

01:11:59.040 --> 01:12:00.780 align:middle line:90%
really good at explaining math?

01:12:00.780 --> 01:12:05.550 align:middle line:84%
And then, we could unbundle
that part of their work out.

01:12:05.550 --> 01:12:07.772 align:middle line:84%
And instead, just have
them focus on the things

01:12:07.772 --> 01:12:10.230 align:middle line:84%
that Sal Khan can't do, which
is putting Band-Aids on kids,

01:12:10.230 --> 01:12:12.000 align:middle line:84%
and grading their feedback,
and talking to their parents,

01:12:12.000 --> 01:12:13.083 align:middle line:90%
and those kinds of things.

01:12:13.083 --> 01:12:16.140 align:middle line:90%
So not crazy at the start of it.

01:12:16.140 --> 01:12:18.850 align:middle line:84%
People have taken this
idea even further,

01:12:18.850 --> 01:12:21.540 align:middle line:84%
which is something like, what
if we just unbundled schools

01:12:21.540 --> 01:12:22.240 align:middle line:90%
entirely?

01:12:22.240 --> 01:12:24.890 align:middle line:84%
Why do we pack all these
different functions in schools?

01:12:24.890 --> 01:12:29.260 align:middle line:90%


01:12:29.260 --> 01:12:32.550 align:middle line:84%
Why don't why don't we let
people gain learning experiences

01:12:32.550 --> 01:12:33.840 align:middle line:90%
wherever they want to?

01:12:33.840 --> 01:12:37.213 align:middle line:84%
There is a great leftist
thinker named Ivan Illich.

01:12:37.213 --> 01:12:39.630 align:middle line:84%
We're going to read some of
his book, Deschooling Society,

01:12:39.630 --> 01:12:42.450 align:middle line:84%
later, which was written at
the dawn of personal computing

01:12:42.450 --> 01:12:45.390 align:middle line:84%
as an incredible prediction
about the future of learning

01:12:45.390 --> 01:12:45.910 align:middle line:90%
online.

01:12:45.910 --> 01:12:47.340 align:middle line:90%
Totally extraordinary.

01:12:47.340 --> 01:12:50.370 align:middle line:84%
But he was very taken by
the ideas of this guy named

01:12:50.370 --> 01:12:53.670 align:middle line:84%
Milton Friedman, who's a
very famous economist, who

01:12:53.670 --> 01:12:56.075 align:middle line:84%
believed really strongly
in the power of markets.

01:12:56.075 --> 01:12:58.450 align:middle line:84%
That governments weren't really
good at solving problems.

01:12:58.450 --> 01:13:00.533 align:middle line:84%
That markets were really
good at solving problems.

01:13:00.533 --> 01:13:02.670 align:middle line:84%
What if we got rid
of schools and just

01:13:02.670 --> 01:13:06.570 align:middle line:84%
had people engage in a
marketplace of learning

01:13:06.570 --> 01:13:07.210 align:middle line:90%
activities?

01:13:07.210 --> 01:13:10.000 align:middle line:84%
What if we unbundled the
different functions of schools?

01:13:10.000 --> 01:13:13.430 align:middle line:84%
Why do you have to take math and
English and physical education

01:13:13.430 --> 01:13:15.630 align:middle line:84%
and sex education from
the same institution?

01:13:15.630 --> 01:13:18.950 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you just let people
buy those learning experiences

01:13:18.950 --> 01:13:21.830 align:middle line:90%
from wherever they want to?

01:13:21.830 --> 01:13:24.320 align:middle line:84%
The horseshoe is
here because this

01:13:24.320 --> 01:13:27.840 align:middle line:84%
is part of the arguments
where people on the left,

01:13:27.840 --> 01:13:29.960 align:middle line:84%
people on the right,
suddenly found themselves

01:13:29.960 --> 01:13:34.790 align:middle line:84%
in of strange convergence
around personalization.

01:13:34.790 --> 01:13:37.100 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll pass this just to--

01:13:37.100 --> 01:13:41.660 align:middle line:84%
so the pedagogical
ideas of personalization

01:13:41.660 --> 01:13:45.900 align:middle line:84%
got connected to a broader
political project--

01:13:45.900 --> 01:13:48.120 align:middle line:84%
which is enormously successful
in the United States,

01:13:48.120 --> 01:13:50.078 align:middle line:84%
particularly in conservative
states right now--

01:13:50.078 --> 01:13:53.540 align:middle line:84%
of trying to refashion
school systems

01:13:53.540 --> 01:13:56.120 align:middle line:84%
with less of a focus on schools
and much more of a focus

01:13:56.120 --> 01:13:59.090 align:middle line:90%
on individual students.

01:13:59.090 --> 01:14:04.520 align:middle line:84%
A very popular idea among people
who advocate for this thing

01:14:04.520 --> 01:14:08.250 align:middle line:84%
is to say, why do we give monies
to schools to educate people?

01:14:08.250 --> 01:14:12.380 align:middle line:84%
What if we took that money and
gave it directly to students?

01:14:12.380 --> 01:14:15.680 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes these systems
were called vouchers.

01:14:15.680 --> 01:14:17.880 align:middle line:84%
Like, don't send people
to public school.

01:14:17.880 --> 01:14:20.240 align:middle line:84%
Give them a voucher to
buy any school they want.

01:14:20.240 --> 01:14:23.750 align:middle line:84%
It turned out the word voucher
was extremely unpopular

01:14:23.750 --> 01:14:26.120 align:middle line:84%
and evoked a voucher
that you would

01:14:26.120 --> 01:14:28.550 align:middle line:84%
get like in an airport
when your flight is

01:14:28.550 --> 01:14:31.160 align:middle line:84%
canceled that doesn't pay for
your hotel and things like that.

01:14:31.160 --> 01:14:33.020 align:middle line:84%
For a while they
experimented with this idea

01:14:33.020 --> 01:14:35.338 align:middle line:90%
of student backpacks.

01:14:35.338 --> 01:14:37.380 align:middle line:84%
Like, the money would go
into a student backpack.

01:14:37.380 --> 01:14:39.810 align:middle line:84%
But that evoked this idea
of backpacks full of cash,

01:14:39.810 --> 01:14:41.570 align:middle line:90%
which was kind of unseemly.

01:14:41.570 --> 01:14:46.340 align:middle line:84%
So the term that
folks use right now

01:14:46.340 --> 01:14:49.790 align:middle line:84%
is to fund students
instead of systems.

01:14:49.790 --> 01:14:51.330 align:middle line:84%
Give money to
individual students.

01:14:51.330 --> 01:14:54.980 align:middle line:84%
These are all the states in 2023
that passed or were considering

01:14:54.980 --> 01:14:56.410 align:middle line:84%
legislation that
would say, we're

01:14:56.410 --> 01:14:57.900 align:middle line:84%
going to take your
property taxes.

01:14:57.900 --> 01:14:59.567 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to
give them to schools.

01:14:59.567 --> 01:15:01.610 align:middle line:84%
We're going to give
them to individual kids.

01:15:01.610 --> 01:15:03.290 align:middle line:84%
And the individual
kids can then use

01:15:03.290 --> 01:15:06.330 align:middle line:84%
those dollars to buy whatever
public school they want.

01:15:06.330 --> 01:15:07.830 align:middle line:90%
Or, to buy a parochial school.

01:15:07.830 --> 01:15:10.490 align:middle line:84%
Or, to pay more money
and buy a private school.

01:15:10.490 --> 01:15:12.680 align:middle line:84%
Or, any kind of
things like that.

01:15:12.680 --> 01:15:15.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Were those funds
given to all students?

01:15:15.590 --> 01:15:17.240 align:middle line:90%
Is there a criteria?

01:15:17.240 --> 01:15:19.100 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: The
ideal, the ideal model

01:15:19.100 --> 01:15:21.080 align:middle line:90%
is that everyone would get them.

01:15:21.080 --> 01:15:24.140 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you believe in
this free market system,

01:15:24.140 --> 01:15:25.970 align:middle line:84%
the only way public
schools should exist

01:15:25.970 --> 01:15:28.875 align:middle line:84%
is if they can
compete in the market

01:15:28.875 --> 01:15:30.000 align:middle line:90%
for other kinds of schools.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:31.705 align:middle line:84%
You can also imagine you
don't even need schools.

01:15:31.705 --> 01:15:33.980 align:middle line:84%
Like, what if you bought
your math from Khan Academy,

01:15:33.980 --> 01:15:36.410 align:middle line:84%
and your health class from
CVS, and your language

01:15:36.410 --> 01:15:39.350 align:middle line:84%
class from Rosetta Stone, and
your sex ed class from the Big

01:15:39.350 --> 01:15:41.780 align:middle line:84%
Valley Creation Science
Museum, and your English class

01:15:41.780 --> 01:15:44.990 align:middle line:84%
from the National Writing
Project, and things like that?

01:15:44.990 --> 01:15:48.050 align:middle line:84%
And this is like a
broader political vision

01:15:48.050 --> 01:15:49.920 align:middle line:84%
of what schooling
could be in the future

01:15:49.920 --> 01:15:51.930 align:middle line:90%
that online learning enabled.

01:15:51.930 --> 01:15:55.203 align:middle line:84%
And to some extent, parts of
it are enormously successful.

01:15:55.203 --> 01:15:57.620 align:middle line:84%
I would say, the pandemic--
because people basically hated

01:15:57.620 --> 01:15:59.330 align:middle line:84%
online learning,
as a whole-- threw

01:15:59.330 --> 01:16:01.580 align:middle line:84%
cold water on a whole
bunch of these things.

01:16:01.580 --> 01:16:03.980 align:middle line:84%
But a thing I want us
to keep coming back

01:16:03.980 --> 01:16:06.590 align:middle line:84%
to as we talk about things,
like personalized learning,

01:16:06.590 --> 01:16:10.260 align:middle line:84%
is that they can be
construed as pedagogies.

01:16:10.260 --> 01:16:12.260 align:middle line:84%
But these pedagogies are
almost always connected

01:16:12.260 --> 01:16:16.580 align:middle line:84%
to broader political movements
about how we organize schools.

01:16:16.580 --> 01:16:19.040 align:middle line:84%
The most political question
you could possibly ask

01:16:19.040 --> 01:16:21.200 align:middle line:84%
is how should we
raise our children?

01:16:21.200 --> 01:16:24.110 align:middle line:84%
In some ways, it seems like an
extremely technical question,

01:16:24.110 --> 01:16:27.450 align:middle line:84%
but it is a deeply moral,
value-laden question as well.

01:16:27.450 --> 01:16:30.740 align:middle line:84%
OK, we will continue
this a little bit,

01:16:30.740 --> 01:16:33.360 align:middle line:84%
and say some more things about
it, and look at some more--

01:16:33.360 --> 01:16:35.630 align:middle line:84%
We'll probably get pretty deep
into the evidence in the paper

01:16:35.630 --> 01:16:37.850 align:middle line:84%
that you read, trying to
get at some of the questions

01:16:37.850 --> 01:16:40.790 align:middle line:84%
that Mari asked before, of what
is the evidence base for how

01:16:40.790 --> 01:16:41.760 align:middle line:90%
well these things work?

01:16:41.760 --> 01:16:44.177 align:middle line:84%
Some of Avril's questions,
like what does it mean to work?

01:16:44.177 --> 01:16:47.780 align:middle line:84%
We'll do a bunch of that with
intelligent tutors on Monday.

01:16:47.780 --> 01:16:49.140 align:middle line:90%
Have a wonderful weekend.

01:16:49.140 --> 01:16:50.720 align:middle line:84%
I look forward to getting
and reading all your papers.

01:16:50.720 --> 01:16:52.762 align:middle line:84%
And if I can help you with
anything, let me know.

01:16:52.762 --> 01:16:55.040 align:middle line:90%
Thanks, everybody.

01:16:55.040 --> 01:17:08.000 align:middle line:90%